var e={"company:giggle":{intro:`Modelled on Google, the web search company that two Stanford PhD students, Larry Page and Sergey Brin, set up in a rented garage in Menlo Park, California. Its engine ranked pages by how many other pages linked to them, and its home page was famously bare: a logo, a search box and two buttons.`,how:`Instead of buying big servers, Google strung together racks of cheap PCs and wrote software that carried on when some of them failed. Its PageRank method treated every link as a vote, weighted by how important the linking page was, which gave far better answers than engines that simply counted words. Search was free to users, and its first money came from licensing the engine to other websites.`,history:`Page and Brin began the BackRub project at Stanford in 1996 and incorporated Google in September 1998 with a $100,000 cheque from Sun co-founder Andy Bechtolsheim. In 2000 it launched AdWords, small text adverts shown beside the results, and from 2002 it sold them by auction and charged only when someone clicked. It floated on the stock market in 2004 and bought YouTube for $1.65 billion in 2006. In 2014 it bought the London AI lab DeepMind, whose AlphaGo program beat the Go champion Lee Sedol in 2016. In 2017 its researchers published the transformer, a new design for language models. In 2023 it merged DeepMind with its Google Brain team as Google DeepMind, which launched the Gemini models that December.`,facts:[`Its first index held about 26 million web pages (1998).`,`Shares were first sold to the public at $85 each (2004).`,`AlphaGo beat Lee Sedol by four games to one in Seoul (2016).`,`Alphabet passed $2 trillion in stock market value (2024).`]},"company:microsloth":{intro:`Modelled on Microsoft, the software firm that childhood friends Bill Gates and Paul Allen founded in Albuquerque, New Mexico, to sell a version of the BASIC programming language for the Altair 8800, one of the first hobby microcomputers. Gates left Harvard to run it, and the name was short for microcomputer software.`,how:`Microsoft built no computers. It wrote programming languages and licensed them to hardware makers, who paid a fee for every copy they shipped, so once a program was written each extra sale cost almost nothing. Gates insisted on keeping the right to license the same software to every rival maker, so the more machines were sold, the more Microsoft earned.`,history:`Gates and Allen founded Microsoft in April 1975 and delivered Altair BASIC to its maker, MITS. In 1980 IBM asked it for an operating system for its coming PC, and Microsoft bought a program called 86-DOS from Seattle Computer Products, renamed it MS-DOS and kept the right to sell it to other makers. Windows arrived in 1985, and the company floated on the stock market in 1986. Windows 95 sold about 7 million copies in its first five weeks in 1995. In 2000 a US judge ordered it split in two for abusing its monopoly, a ruling overturned on appeal in 2001. In 2019 it invested $1 billion in OpenAI and agreed to supply its computing power.`,facts:[`Altair BASIC fitted into 4 kilobytes of memory (1975).`,`Microsoft was worth over $600 billion, the most of any company (1999).`,`Microsoft had committed about $13 billion to OpenAI by 2023.`,`Its stock market value briefly passed $4 trillion (2025).`]},"company:amazoom":{intro:`Modelled on Amazon, the online shop Jeff Bezos started in the garage of a rented house in Bellevue, near Seattle, after leaving a Wall Street hedge fund. It began with books, because millions of titles were in print and no real shop could stock them all, and it was first registered under the name Cadabra.`,how:`Amazon held little stock at first: it took orders on its website, bought the books from wholesalers and posted them out, so it could list far more titles than any bookshop. Bezos ran it for growth rather than profit, ploughing every dollar back into lower prices, faster delivery and new product lines, and told shareholders to judge it over the long term.`,history:`Bezos founded the company in July 1994. It opened its website in July 1995, renamed after the world's largest river, and floated on the stock market in 1997. It made its first full-year profit in 2003, after surviving the dot-com crash. In 2006 it began renting out its own computing power as Amazon Web Services, with the S3 storage and EC2 rented-server services, the first big public cloud. It launched the Kindle e-reader in 2007. Between 2023 and 2024 it invested $8 billion in the AI lab Anthropic and offered its own Trainium chips for training AI models.`,facts:[`Amazon first sold shares to the public at $18 each (1997).`,`Its Echo speaker brought the Alexa voice assistant into homes (2014).`,`Amazon employed more than 1.5 million people (2021).`,`Amazon Web Services earned about $108 billion of revenue (2024).`]},"company:mehta":{intro:`Modelled on Meta, the company behind Facebook, which Harvard student Mark Zuckerberg launched from his dormitory as an online directory where students posted their names, photos and interests. At first it was open only to people with a university email address, starting with Harvard and spreading campus by campus.`,how:`Facebook was free: users filled it with profiles, photos and messages, and the company earned money by showing them adverts. Because people used their real names and listed their friends, it knew far more about its users than most websites did, which let advertisers aim at exactly the people they wanted.`,history:`Zuckerberg and his roommates launched TheFacebook in February 2004 and moved it to Palo Alto, California, that summer. It opened to anyone over 13 in 2006 and added the News Feed the same year. In 2012 it bought the photo app Instagram for $1 billion and floated, raising $16 billion, and in 2014 it paid $19 billion for the messaging app WhatsApp. In 2018 the Cambridge Analytica scandal revealed that data from up to 87 million users had been taken for political targeting. It renamed itself Meta in 2021 to bet on virtual reality, and in 2023 it began releasing its Llama language models for anyone to download and run.`,facts:[`Facebook passed 1 billion monthly users (2012).`,`WhatsApp had about 55 staff when Facebook paid $19 billion for it (2014).`,`More than 3 billion people used Meta's apps every day (2024).`]},"company:pear":{intro:`Modelled on Apple, the computer company Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak started in the Jobs family garage in Los Altos, California, to sell Wozniak's home-built computer board, the Apple I. Jobs was the salesman and Wozniak the engineer, and they raised their first money by selling a van and a pocket calculator.`,how:`Apple aimed to make computers that ordinary people could use. It designed the circuit board, the case and the software together and sold finished machines through shops rather than kits for hobbyists, and that control over every part let it charge more than rivals for a machine that looked and felt better.`,history:`Jobs, Wozniak and Ronald Wayne founded Apple on 1 April 1976 and sold the Apple I for $666.66. The Apple II of 1977, with colour graphics and a smart plastic case, made it rich. The Macintosh of 1984 brought the mouse and on-screen windows to the mass market, but Jobs was forced out in 1985. Close to bankruptcy, Apple bought his company NeXT in 1997 and brought him back, and the colourful iMac of 1998 began its recovery. The iPod music player of 2001 and the iTunes Store of 2003 made it a force in music. In 2007 it launched the iPhone, which combined a phone, an iPod and the internet in one touchscreen device.`,facts:[`Its share sale was the biggest since Ford's in 1956 (1980).`,`The first iPhone cost $499 with 4 GB of storage (2007).`,`Apple became the first US company worth $1 trillion (2018).`,`Its stock market value touched $3 trillion (2022).`]},"company:ibm":{intro:`Modelled on IBM, the American business-machine giant that grew from a merger of firms making punched-card tabulators, time clocks, shop scales and meat slicers. Its punched-card machines sorted and totalled the records of governments, railways and insurers, and its salesmen were famous for their dark suits and white shirts.`,how:`IBM rented out its machines rather than selling them and sold the punched cards they used up, earning steady rent and supplies year after year. Its salesmen learned their customers' businesses and its engineers kept the machines running, so big organisations seldom dared to switch to a rival.`,history:`The financier Charles Flint merged four companies into the Computing-Tabulating-Recording Company in 1911, and Thomas Watson, who took charge in 1914, renamed it International Business Machines in 1924; in 1936 it won the job of keeping the records of the new US Social Security system. In 1964 it bet about $5 billion on System/360, the first family of mainframes that could all run the same programs. In 1981 it launched the IBM PC, built from parts bought from others, including Intel's processor and Microsoft's DOS. It lost $8 billion in 1993, and its new chief Lou Gerstner turned it towards services. Its Deep Blue computer beat the chess champion Garry Kasparov in 1997. In 2019 IBM bought the Linux company Red Hat for $34 billion.`,facts:[`IBM employed about 400,000 people (1985).`,`Its loss of $8 billion was then a record for a US company (1993).`,`Deep Blue won its rematch with Kasparov by 3.5 games to 2.5 (1997).`,`IBM sold its PC business to Lenovo for about $1.75 billion (2005).`]},"company:obstacle":{intro:`Modelled on Oracle, the database company that Larry Ellison, Bob Miner and Ed Oates started in Santa Clara, California, under the name Software Development Laboratories. They set out to sell a commercial version of the relational database, an idea published by IBM researcher Edgar Codd that IBM itself was slow to bring to market.`,how:`Oracle sold licences for database software that kept a company's records in tables and answered questions written in SQL, a query language IBM had devised, plus yearly support fees that kept coming long after the sale. It aimed to run on many makes of computer, not only IBM's, and Ellison was a fierce salesman who made bold claims.`,history:`The three founders started the firm in 1977 with about $2,000. In 1979 it shipped Oracle Version 2, one of the first SQL databases on sale; there had never been a Version 1. It floated on the stock market in 1986, a day before Microsoft. In 2005 it bought the business software maker PeopleSoft after a hostile bid. In 2010 it bought Sun Microsystems, gaining the Java language. In 2025 huge contracts to build AI data centres for OpenAI briefly made Ellison the richest person in the world.`,facts:[`Oracle paid $10.3 billion for PeopleSoft (2005).`,`Oracle bought Sun Microsystems for $7.4 billion (2010).`,`Its shares jumped about 36% in a day on AI cloud orders (2025).`]},"company:closedai":{intro:`Modelled on OpenAI, the artificial intelligence lab founded in San Francisco as a non-profit, with backers who pledged $1 billion, to make sure that AI smarter than humans would benefit everyone. Its founders included Sam Altman, Elon Musk, Greg Brockman and Ilya Sutskever, and it promised to share its research openly.`,how:`OpenAI began as a research lab funded by donations: it hired leading researchers, published papers and released code. It planned work on game-playing agents, robotics and language, all of which needed ever more computing power, the costliest ingredient in modern AI.`,history:`OpenAI was announced in December 2015. In 2019 it created a capped-profit company to raise money, and Microsoft invested $1 billion. Its GPT-3 language model of 2020 showed that a large enough model could write passable text on almost any topic. ChatGPT, launched on 30 November 2022, reached about 100 million users within two months, then the fastest growth of any consumer app. In November 2023 its board fired chief executive Sam Altman, only to reinstate him five days later after most of the staff threatened to quit. In 2025 it reorganised as a public benefit corporation controlled by its non-profit and was valued at $500 billion.`,facts:[`GPT-3 had 175 billion parameters (2020).`,`ChatGPT had about 800 million weekly users (2025).`,`Microsoft had committed about $13 billion to OpenAI by 2023.`]},"company:entropic":{intro:`Modelled on Anthropic, the AI safety company founded in San Francisco by the siblings Dario and Daniela Amodei and other former OpenAI staff. It set out to build large AI models while studying how to make them reliable, understandable and safe, and it was registered as a public benefit corporation.`,how:`Anthropic's plan was to stay at the frontier of AI research so that its safety work applied to the most capable systems, paying the huge computing bills by selling access to its models to businesses and developers. Alongside training models, it studies what goes on inside them and how to steer their behaviour.`,history:`Anthropic was founded in 2021 and raised $124 million in its first funding round that May. In December 2022 it published Constitutional AI, a method for training a model to follow a written set of principles, and in March 2023 it released its first Claude model. Between 2023 and 2024 Amazon invested $8 billion, and Google also put in several billion dollars. The Claude 3 family arrived in March 2024, and in 2025 it launched Claude Code, a tool that writes and edits software from the command line. In September 2025 it raised $13 billion at a valuation of $183 billion.`,facts:[`Claude could read about 75,000 words in one go (2023).`,`Its yearly revenue rate passed $5 billion (2025).`]},"company:exai":{intro:`Modelled on xAI, the artificial intelligence company Elon Musk set up to build what he called a maximally truth-seeking AI, together with X, the social network formerly called Twitter, which Musk had bought. Its chatbot, Grok, was built into X and drew on its live stream of posts.`,how:`xAI trains large language models on its own giant clusters of NVIDIA chips and sells them through X subscriptions, its own apps and an API for developers. X earned most of its money from adverts shown between posts, plus paid subscriptions that gave accounts a verified tick.`,history:`Twitter was launched in 2006 by Jack Dorsey, Noah Glass, Biz Stone and Evan Williams. Musk bought it for $44 billion in October 2022 and renamed it X in July 2023. He founded xAI in March 2023 and released the first Grok that November. In 2024 xAI built its Colossus supercomputer in Memphis, Tennessee, with 100,000 NVIDIA H100 chips, in about four months. In March 2025 xAI took over X in an all-share deal, and in February 2026 Musk's rocket company SpaceX acquired the combined firm, valuing xAI at about $250 billion.`,facts:[`Musk paid $54.20 a share for Twitter (2022).`,`X was valued at $33 billion when it merged into xAI (2025).`,`Colossus was built in 122 days (2024).`]},"company:deepcheap":{intro:`Modelled on DeepSeek, the Chinese AI lab in Hangzhou founded by Liang Wenfeng and paid for by High-Flyer, the quantitative hedge fund he co-founded. Rather than chasing investors, it used the fund's own stock of NVIDIA chips to pursue artificial general intelligence, hiring mostly young graduates from Chinese universities.`,how:`DeepSeek released its models with open weights, free for anyone to download, and charged very low prices for its API. It set out to squeeze more out of fewer, export-restricted chips with clever engineering, such as mixture-of-experts models, which use only a slice of the network for each word, and low-precision arithmetic that needs less memory.`,history:`High-Flyer, which Liang started with two university classmates, began trading with AI models and had bought about 10,000 NVIDIA A100 chips by 2021, before US export controls arrived. Liang spun out DeepSeek in July 2023 and released its first open models that November. Its DeepSeek-V2 of May 2024 cut prices so far that it set off a price war among Chinese AI firms. DeepSeek-V3, released in December 2024, matched leading Western models and reported a final training run costing only $5.6 million. Its reasoning model R1, released free in January 2025, topped the US App Store and helped knock about $589 billion off NVIDIA's value on 27 January 2025.`,facts:[`DeepSeek-V3 had 671 billion parameters but used only 37 billion for each word (2024).`,`V3's final training run used 2,048 NVIDIA H800 chips for about two months (2024).`,`NVIDIA's one-day loss of about $589 billion was the largest in history (2025).`]},"company:zippy":{intro:`Modelled on Zhipu AI, a Beijing company spun out of Tsinghua University's Knowledge Engineering Group by professors Tang Jie and Li Juanzi. It began by turning the lab's research on knowledge graphs and its AMiner search engine for scientists and papers into products, and set out to build China's own large pre-trained language models.`,how:`Zhipu earned money from research tools and data services for universities, companies and government bodies, which wanted Chinese-built AI. Its language models are trained in a style it calls General Language Model, which mixes filling in blanks with writing the next word.`,history:`Zhipu was founded in Beijing in 2019. In 2022 it released GLM-130B, an open bilingual model with 130 billion parameters, and in March 2023 the small ChatGLM-6B, which could run on a single gaming graphics card. In January 2025 the US put it on its Entity List, restricting its access to American technology. In 2025 it took the name Z.ai abroad and released GLM-4.5, an open model aimed at coding and agents. On 8 January 2026 it listed on the Hong Kong stock exchange, raising about $560 million, the first of China's new AI model makers to go public.`,facts:[`GLM-130B was trained on over 400 billion words and word pieces in English and Chinese (2022).`,`Zhipu's revenue was 191 million yuan in the first half of 2025, against a loss of 2.36 billion yuan.`,`Its Hong Kong shares were offered at HK$116.20 each (2026).`]},"company:alibabble":{intro:`Modelled on Alibaba, the Chinese internet company that former English teacher Jack Ma and 17 friends started in his flat in Hangzhou. Its website listed Chinese manufacturers so that small businesses around the world could find suppliers and trade with them online.`,how:`Alibaba.com was a business-to-business marketplace: sellers paid yearly membership fees for their listings and for better placement, while buyers searched for free. Ma promised to help China's small exporters, who had few other ways to reach foreign customers, and the firm grew on very little money.`,history:`Ma and his partners founded Alibaba in 1999 with about $60,000 of their own money. In 2003 it launched Taobao, a free consumer marketplace that drove eBay out of China, and in 2004 the Alipay payment service. Yahoo paid $1 billion for 40% of the company in 2005. Its New York share sale of 2014 raised $25 billion, the largest ever at the time. After Ma criticised China's financial regulators in 2020, the share sale of its Ant Group payments arm was halted, and in 2021 Alibaba was fined a record 18.2 billion yuan. In 2023 it launched its Qwen language models and began releasing many of them with open weights.`,facts:[`Alibaba Cloud was started (2009).`,`Its Singles' Day shopping festival sold $30.8 billion of goods in a day (2018).`,`Developers had built more than 100,000 models from Qwen on Hugging Face (2025).`]},"company:huawow":{intro:`Modelled on Huawei, the Shenzhen company that former army engineer Ren Zhengfei started with 21,000 yuan to resell telephone exchange equipment imported through Hong Kong. Its first customers were rural telephone offices that the big foreign suppliers ignored, and Ren aimed to design China's own exchanges.`,how:`Huawei won customers on price and service: it sold network gear cheaply, sent engineers to live near its customers and fixed problems fast, and it poured a large share of its income into research. Ren kept the company private, sharing ownership with employees rather than outside investors.`,history:`Ren founded Huawei in 1987. In 1993 it launched the C&C08, a digital exchange that became its first big success. By 2012 it had overtaken Ericsson as the largest maker of telecom network equipment in the world. US sanctions from 2019 cut it off from Google's Android services and, from 2020, from TSMC's chip factories, so it built its own HarmonyOS system and Ascend AI chips. Even so, its phones briefly outsold Samsung's worldwide in 2020. In August 2023 its Mate 60 Pro surprised Washington with a 7-nanometre Kirin chip made in China by SMIC, and that year it also launched its Pangu AI models for industry.`,facts:[`Huawei spent about 165 billion yuan on research, nearly a quarter of its revenue (2023).`,`It employed about 207,000 people (2023).`]},"company:edison":{intro:`Modelled on Tesla, the electric car company that engineers Martin Eberhard and Marc Tarpenning founded in California to prove that electric cars could be fast and desirable. Named after the inventor Nikola Tesla, it planned to start with an expensive sports car and use the profits to build cheaper models.`,how:`Tesla packed thousands of small lithium-ion cells, the kind used in laptops, into its battery packs, because they were cheap and plentiful, and it designed its own motors, electronics and software. It planned to sell straight to buyers rather than through car dealers.`,history:`Eberhard and Tarpenning incorporated Tesla in July 2003. Elon Musk led its first funding round in 2004 and became chairman. The Roadster sports car went on sale in 2008, and the company floated in 2010. The Model S saloon arrived in 2012, and from 2014 it was fitted with hardware for Autopilot driver assistance. Its cheaper Model 3 went on sale in 2017, and in 2018 it was the world's bestselling electric car. In June 2025 Tesla launched a small robotaxi service in Austin, Texas, and wound down its Dojo training supercomputer.`,facts:[`Musk put $6.5 million into Tesla's first funding round (2004).`,`The Roadster's battery held 6,831 laptop-style cells (2008).`,`Its stock market value passed $1 trillion (2021).`,`Tesla delivered about 1.8 million cars (2023).`]},"company:waymore":{intro:`Modelled on Waymo, the self-driving car company spun out of Google's robot-car project into Google's parent group, Alphabet. Its cars carried spinning laser scanners, radar and cameras and aimed to drive with no human at the wheel, starting with taxi rides in sunny American suburbs.`,how:`Waymo's cars build a 3D picture of their surroundings with lidar, which measures distance with laser pulses, and compare it with detailed maps made in advance. Software trained on millions of miles of driving predicts what every car, cyclist and pedestrian will do next. Waymo planned to earn its money from driverless taxis.`,history:`Google began the project in 2009 under Sebastian Thrun, whose Stanford team had won a desert race for robot cars run by the US military's DARPA agency in 2005. In October 2015 its bubble-shaped Firefly prototype gave the first fully driverless ride on public roads, to a blind man in Austin, Texas. The project became Waymo in December 2016, and in December 2018 it launched Waymo One, a paid taxi service around Phoenix, Arizona. In 2020 it opened rides with no safety driver to the public in Phoenix. In 2024 it expanded to everyone in San Francisco and Los Angeles, and by 2025 it was giving about 250,000 paid rides a week.`,facts:[`Waymo raised $5.6 billion in a single funding round (2024).`,`Its cars passed 100 million fully driverless miles (2025).`]},"company:mistrial":{intro:`Modelled on Mistral AI, the Paris start-up founded by three French researchers: Arthur Mensch from Google DeepMind, and Guillaume Lample and Timothée Lacroix from Meta. Named after the strong cold wind of southern France, it aimed to give Europe a frontier AI lab of its own.`,how:`Mistral built small, efficient language models and released many with open weights under permissive licences, so anyone could run them. It planned to earn money by selling its largest models and private installations to businesses and governments that wanted their AI kept under European control.`,history:`Mistral was founded in April 2023 and in June 2023 raised a record €105 million seed round before it had any product. In September 2023 it released Mistral 7B as a free download, announced with nothing but a torrent link on social media, and in December 2023 it released Mixtral 8x7B, an open mixture-of-experts model. In February 2024 it launched its Le Chat assistant and signed a partnership with Microsoft. In September 2025 the Dutch chip-machine maker ASML led a €1.7 billion round that valued Mistral at €11.7 billion.`,facts:[`Mistral 7B had 7.3 billion parameters (2023).`,`Its €105 million seed was the largest in European history (2023).`,`ASML took a stake of about 11% (2025).`]},"company:midlife":{intro:`Modelled on Midjourney, the small independent research lab in San Francisco led by David Holz, co-founder of the hand-tracking company Leap Motion. It set out to build an AI that turns a typed description into a picture, with a tiny team, no outside investors and the stated aim of expanding the imaginative powers of the human species.`,how:`Midjourney ran its image generator through a chat server on Discord, where users typed a prompt and watched pictures appear in public channels, learning tricks from each other. It charged monthly subscriptions, so it earned money from the day its service opened instead of relying on venture capital.`,history:`Holz founded Midjourney in 2021. It opened a public beta on Discord in July 2022. In August 2022 a picture made with it, Théâtre D'opéra Spatial, won the digital art prize at the Colorado State Fair and set off a fierce argument about AI art. Its version 5 of March 2023 made realistic photographs, including a viral fake picture of the Pope in a white puffer jacket. In June 2025 Disney and Universal sued it for copying their characters, and that month it launched its first video model. In August 2025 Meta agreed to license its image technology.`,facts:[`Midjourney reportedly earned about $200 million with around 40 staff (2023).`,`It took no venture capital funding at all (2023).`]},"company:typosafe":{intro:`Modelled on TypeSafe AI, an American AI start-up led by Diogo Almeida, a former OpenAI researcher who worked on the training behind ChatGPT. It builds what it calls System One models, named after the fast, intuitive kind of thinking described by the psychologist Daniel Kahneman, instead of chatbots that write answers word by word.`,how:`A System One model is given a block of information and a set of typed questions, such as yes or no, a choice from a list or a score, and answers them all at once with calibrated probabilities: its stated confidence matches how often it is right. With no text to write, answers take well under a second and cost a tiny fraction of a chatbot's, which suits routing, moderation and classification.`,history:`TypeSafe AI came out of stealth in September 2026, opening early access to its first model, Jev, and announcing a $40 million seed round led by the venture firm DCVC at a $200 million valuation. TypeSafe credited Jev's speed to a training method it called Reinforcement Learning for Calibrated Decisions, and said it had been trained on synthetic data over two years before its 2026 launch. In October 2026, about three weeks after launch, it raised about $870 million in a round led by Andreessen Horowitz, at a valuation of $7.5 billion.`,facts:[`Jev answered in 70 to 500 milliseconds (2026).`,`Jev cost $0.042 per million input tokens, with output free (2026).`,`Jev was reportedly named after the economist William Stanley Jevons (2026).`]},"company:sueno":{intro:`Modelled on Suno, the Cambridge, Massachusetts, start-up whose AI writes complete songs, with vocals, lyrics and instruments, from a short text description. Its four founders had worked together at Kensho, an AI firm for financial data, and several of them were musicians as well as machine-learning researchers.`,how:`Suno's models treat audio like language: sound is chopped into small tokens and a transformer predicts the next one, much as a chatbot predicts the next word. Users get a few free songs a day and pay a subscription for more songs and for the right to use them commercially.`,history:`Mikey Shulman, Georg Kucsko, Martin Camacho and Keenan Freyberg founded Suno in 2022. In April 2023 it released Bark, an open-source text-to-speech model, and in December 2023 its song maker was built into Microsoft's Copilot. In May 2024 it raised $125 million, and in June 2024 the three major record companies, Universal, Sony and Warner, sued it for training on their recordings. In November 2025 it settled with Warner Music, agreeing to launch licensed models, and raised $250 million at a $2.45 billion valuation.`,facts:[`Its version 3 could make songs up to two minutes long (2024).`,`The labels sought up to $150,000 for each song they said was copied (2024).`]},"company:yoodio":{intro:`Modelled on Udio, the New York music start-up founded by former Google DeepMind researchers, whose AI turns a text prompt into a song with singing. It launched with backing from the venture firm Andreessen Horowitz and from musicians including will.i.am and Common.`,how:`Udio generates a short clip at a time, which users extend, remix and edit into a full track, choosing genres, moods and lyrics. It offers a free tier with a monthly allowance and paid plans with more songs and faster generation.`,history:`David Ding and colleagues from Google DeepMind founded Udio in 2023, and it opened to the public in April 2024 with a $10 million seed round. In May 2024 a comic song made with it, BBL Drizzy, went viral and was remixed by the producer Metro Boomin. In June 2024 the major labels sued Udio alongside Suno for training on their recordings. In October 2025 it settled with Universal Music, announced a licensed platform and stopped users downloading their songs, and in November 2025 it settled with Warner Music too.`,facts:[`Udio's first clips were about 32 seconds long (2024).`,`Its seed round raised $10 million (2024).`]},"company:elevenlaughs":{intro:`Modelled on ElevenLabs, the voice AI company founded by two Polish childhood friends, Piotr Dąbkowski, a former Google engineer, and Mati Staniszewski, a former Palantir strategist. They were inspired by the flat, single-voice dubbing of foreign films on Polish television and set out to make machine speech sound human in any language.`,how:`ElevenLabs sells text-to-speech that reads text aloud with natural rhythm and emotion, voice cloning from a short recording, and dubbing that translates a speaker's words while keeping their voice. Customers range from audiobook publishers and video makers to firms building voice assistants, and they pay by subscription or by the amount of text spoken.`,history:`Dąbkowski and Staniszewski founded ElevenLabs in 2022. Its beta opened in January 2023, and within weeks users of the 4chan forum cloned celebrities' voices to make them say offensive things, so the company tightened its safeguards. In January 2024 it became a unicorn, a start-up valued at over $1 billion. In January 2025 it raised $180 million at a $3.3 billion valuation, and a share sale for staff in September 2025 valued it at $6.6 billion.`,facts:[`ElevenLabs was valued at $1.1 billion (2024).`,`It was valued at $6.6 billion (2025).`]},"company:spaceex":{intro:`Modelled on SpaceX, the rocket company Elon Musk founded in El Segundo, near Los Angeles, with money from selling the online payments firm PayPal. Its goal was to cut the cost of reaching space so far that people could one day live on Mars, starting with a small, cheap rocket called Falcon 1.`,how:`SpaceX made almost everything itself, from engines to flight computers, instead of buying parts from the usual aerospace contractors, and designed its rockets to be built quickly and cheaply. It planned to earn money by launching satellites for companies and governments at a fraction of the usual price.`,history:`Musk founded SpaceX in 2002. Its Falcon 1 reached orbit on its fourth try in September 2008, and that December NASA gave it a $1.6 billion contract to carry cargo to the International Space Station. In May 2012 its Dragon capsule became the first private spacecraft to reach the station. In December 2015 a Falcon 9 first stage flew back and landed upright, ready to be reused. It began launching Starlink internet satellites in 2019, and in May 2020 Crew Dragon carried NASA astronauts, the first crewed flight by a private company. In October 2024 its launch tower caught a returning Starship booster, and in February 2026 SpaceX took over Musk's AI company xAI.`,facts:[`Falcon 1's first three launches failed (2006 to 2008).`,`Starlink passed 4 million customers (2024).`,`SpaceX made more than 130 launches in a single year (2024).`,`It had more than 8,000 Starlink satellites in orbit (2025).`]},"company:envydia":{intro:`Modelled on NVIDIA, the graphics chip company that Jensen Huang, Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem planned over meals at a Denny's restaurant in San Jose, California. They bet that personal computers would need dedicated chips to draw 3D graphics for games and multimedia.`,how:`NVIDIA designed chips but owned no factories: it paid chip foundries to manufacture them and sold them to the companies that built graphics cards and PCs. That left it free to spend its money on engineers, and it aimed to win by bringing out new generations of chip faster than its rivals.`,history:`NVIDIA was founded in April 1993. Its first chip, the NV1 of 1995, flopped because it drew curved surfaces when games were moving to triangles. The RIVA 128 of 1997 saved it, and in 1999 it floated and launched the GeForce 256, which it marketed as the first GPU, or graphics processing unit. In 2006 it released CUDA, letting programmers use its chips for general calculations. In 2012 the AlexNet image recogniser, trained on two of its gaming cards, set off the deep-learning boom. Its H100 chip of 2022 became the workhorse of AI training, and in 2023 NVIDIA passed $1 trillion in stock market value.`,facts:[`NVIDIA was started with about $40,000 (1993).`,`The GeForce 256 had about 23 million transistors (1999).`,`Data-centre chips earned it about $115 billion in the year to January 2025.`,`It became the first company worth $5 trillion (2025).`]},"company:amdeez":{intro:`Modelled on AMD, Advanced Micro Devices, which Jerry Sanders and seven colleagues from Fairchild Semiconductor started in Sunnyvale, California. Sanders was a flamboyant salesman, and the firm began by making copies of other companies' logic chips, tested to stricter military standards.`,how:`AMD made chips in its own factories and won business as a reliable second supplier for parts other firms had designed, because big buyers wanted at least two sources for every component. Sanders' motto was that people came first, and products and profit would follow.`,history:`AMD was founded on 1 May 1969. In 1982 IBM had it licensed as a second source for Intel's x86 processors used in the IBM PC. Its Athlon chip of 1999 outran Intel's Pentium III, and in March 2000 it was the first to reach 1 gigahertz. In 2003 it brought out AMD64, the 64-bit extension of the x86 design that Intel itself then adopted. In 2006 it bought the graphics maker ATI for $5.4 billion. Under Lisa Su, chief executive from 2014, its Zen processors of 2017 won back customers.`,facts:[`Short of cash, it spun off its factories as GlobalFoundries (2009).`,`AMD's stock market value passed Intel's for the first time (2020).`,`AMD paid about $49 billion in shares for the chip designer Xilinx (2022).`,`Its MI300X AI chip took on NVIDIA in data centres (2023).`]},"company:groqnroll":{intro:`Modelled on Groq, the Silicon Valley chip start-up founded by Jonathan Ross, who had begun Google's tensor processing unit, the TPU, as a side project. Its chip was designed from scratch to run trained AI models as fast and as predictably as possible.`,how:`Groq's chip has none of the usual hardware for juggling tasks: its compiler plans in advance exactly when every piece of data moves and every calculation happens, so each answer takes the same time. It keeps the model in fast memory on the chip itself, spread across many chips, instead of in slower external memory, trading capacity for speed.`,history:`Ross and other former Google engineers founded Groq in 2016. Its first chip, the Tensor Streaming Processor, appeared in 2019. As chatbots took off in 2023, Groq renamed it the LPU, or language processing unit. In February 2024 demos of its GroqCloud service answering at hundreds of words a second went viral, and in August 2024 it raised $640 million. In February 2025 Saudi Arabia committed $1.5 billion to expand its data centres there, and in September 2025 it raised $750 million at a $6.9 billion valuation. On 24 December 2025 NVIDIA agreed to license Groq's technology for a reported $20 billion and hired Ross and other leaders, while Groq stayed independent.`,facts:[`Each Groq chip held about 230 megabytes of memory on the chip itself (2020).`,`GroqCloud ran the Mixtral model at about 500 tokens a second (2024).`,`NVIDIA's licensing deal was reported at about $20 billion (2025).`]},"company:cerebrass":{intro:`Modelled on Cerebras Systems, the Silicon Valley chip company founded by Andrew Feldman, Gary Lauterbach and other veterans of SeaMicro, a server start-up they had sold to AMD. Its radical idea was to build one chip the size of a whole silicon wafer, instead of cutting the wafer into hundreds of small chips.`,how:`A wafer-scale chip keeps a neural network's calculations and memory on one piece of silicon, so data never has to crawl between separate chips. Cerebras designed ways to route around the faulty spots every wafer has, and to feed power and cooling to the giant chip, which it sold inside complete systems.`,history:`Cerebras was founded in 2016. In August 2019 it unveiled its first Wafer Scale Engine, with 1.2 trillion transistors on a square of silicon about 21.5 centimetres across. The WSE-2 followed in 2021. From 2023 it built the Condor Galaxy supercomputers with G42, an AI company from Abu Dhabi that was also its biggest customer. In March 2024 it launched the WSE-3, with 4 trillion transistors, and that year it opened a fast cloud service for chatbots and filed to float on the stock market. In October 2025 it withdrew the listing after raising $1.1 billion privately at an $8.1 billion valuation.`,facts:[`The first Wafer Scale Engine had 400,000 cores (2019).`,`The WSE-3 had 900,000 cores (2024).`,`G42 made up 87% of its revenue in the first half of 2024.`]},"company:intol":{intro:`Modelled on Intel, the chip company that Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore founded in Mountain View, California, after leaving Fairchild Semiconductor, which they had helped create. The name was short for integrated electronics, and their plan was to replace the magnetic-core memory inside computers with memory chips made of silicon.`,how:`Intel designed chips and made them in its own factories, pushing to cram ever more transistors onto each one. Once a chip design worked it could be made in huge numbers, so the firm aimed to be first with each new generation and charge high prices before rivals caught up.`,history:`Noyce and Moore founded Intel in July 1968, with Andy Grove as their first hire. Its 1103 of 1970 was the first big-selling DRAM memory chip, and in 1971 it launched the 4004, the first commercial microprocessor, built for the Japanese calculator maker Busicom. IBM chose its 8088 processor for the IBM PC in 1981. In 1985 Intel gave up memory chips to Japanese rivals and bet everything on processors. Its Intel Inside adverts of 1991 and the Pentium of 1993 made it a household name, and it was the world's biggest chipmaker from 1992. After years of delays in shrinking its transistors, it lost $18.7 billion in 2024, and in August 2025 the US government took a stake of about 10%.`,facts:[`The 4004 had 2,300 transistors (1971).`,`A flaw in the Pentium's division cost Intel $475 million (1994).`,`Intel's revenue was about $53 billion (2024).`]},"company:tsmc":{intro:`Modelled on TSMC, the Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company, founded in Hsinchu by Morris Chang, a former Texas Instruments executive invited home by Taiwan's government. Backed by the government and the Dutch electronics firm Philips, it was the first company set up only to make chips that other companies had designed.`,how:`TSMC designed no chips of its own, so it never competed with its customers: it took their designs and made them in its factories, charging by the wafer. That let small chip designers with no factories compete with giants, while TSMC spread the cost of ever more expensive factories across many customers.`,history:`Chang founded TSMC in 1987. It listed its shares in New York in 1997. In 2014 it began making the processors in Apple's iPhones. In 2018 it led the industry to 7-nanometre chips while Intel struggled to shrink its transistors. In 2020 it began mass-producing 5-nanometre chips and announced its first factory in Arizona. By 2024 it made almost all of the world's most advanced chips, including NVIDIA's AI processors, and in March 2025 it promised to invest $165 billion in the United States.`,facts:[`TSMC held more than 60% of the foundry market (2024).`,`TSMC's revenue was about US$90 billion (2024).`,`Its stock market value passed $1 trillion (2024).`]},"company:samsong":{intro:`Modelled on Samsung Electronics, set up in Suwon, South Korea, as part of the Samsung group, a family-run trading and industrial empire founded by Lee Byung-chul. It began by assembling black-and-white televisions and radios with Japanese partners, aiming to build up Korea's own electronics industry.`,how:`As part of a chaebol, a family conglomerate with close ties to the government, Samsung could pour profits from other businesses into huge, long-term bets. It learned by licensing foreign designs, then built bigger factories faster than anyone else and sold in vast volumes.`,history:`Lee Byung-chul founded Samsung as a trading company in Daegu in 1938, and Samsung Electronics followed in 1969. In 1983 Lee announced that Samsung would make memory chips, and by the end of that year it had built a 64-kilobit DRAM. By 1992 it was the world's largest maker of DRAM. Its Galaxy S phone of 2010 helped make it the world's biggest smartphone maker by 2012. In 2016 exploding Galaxy Note 7 batteries forced a costly worldwide recall. In 2022 it began making the first 3-nanometre chips with a new gate-all-around transistor, but in 2024 it fell behind SK hynix in the high-bandwidth memory used by AI chips.`,facts:[`The Galaxy Note 7 recall cost Samsung more than $5 billion (2016).`,`Samsung Electronics' sales were about 300 trillion won (2024).`]},"company:highnix":{intro:`Modelled on SK hynix, which began as Hyundai Electronics, the chip arm of the South Korean Hyundai group that its founder Chung Ju-yung set up to join the boom in memory chips. It started almost from scratch, licensing designs from abroad and building factories in Icheon.`,how:`Memory makers build identical DRAM chips by the billion, so profits depend on making them smaller and cheaper than rivals do. Prices swing wildly with supply and demand, bringing deep losses in bad years and huge profits in good ones, so only firms with deep pockets survive.`,history:`Hyundai Electronics was founded in 1983. In 1999 the Korean government made it take over LG Semicon after the Asian financial crisis, and in 2001 it was spun off as Hynix, crushed by debts. The SK group, a telecoms and energy conglomerate, bought it in 2012 and renamed it SK hynix. In 2013 it built the first high-bandwidth memory, or HBM, which stacks DRAM chips on top of each other, with AMD. Its HBM3 became the main memory for NVIDIA's AI chips in 2022. In early 2025 it passed Samsung in DRAM sales for the first time.`,facts:[`It agreed to buy Intel's flash memory business for $9 billion (2020).`,`It made a record operating profit of about 23.5 trillion won (2024).`]},"company:mikron":{intro:`Modelled on Micron Technology, the memory chip company founded in Boise, Idaho, by Ward Parkinson, his twin brother Joe, Dennis Wilson and Doug Pitman, who started in the basement of a dentist's office. It began by designing memory chips for another firm before deciding to make its own, an unusual business for a state known for potatoes.`,how:`Micron made DRAM memory chips and competed on cost, designing small chips that needed fewer manufacturing steps so it could survive the price crashes that regularly hit the memory market. It kept its factory in Idaho, where land and power were cheap.`,history:`Micron was founded in 1978. The Idaho potato billionaire J R Simplot backed it in 1980, when it began building its first factory. In 1985 it accused Japanese makers of selling memory below cost, which helped bring about a chip trade deal between the US and Japan in 1986. In 2013 it bought the bankrupt Japanese DRAM maker Elpida. In 2024 the US government awarded it $6.1 billion under the CHIPS Act for new factories in Idaho and New York. Demand for its high-bandwidth memory for AI chips sent its sales to a record in 2025, and that December it said it would stop selling its Crucial memory to consumers.`,facts:[`Micron's first product was a 64-kilobit DRAM (1981).`,`It bought Texas Instruments' memory chip business (1998).`,`Its revenue was about $37 billion in its 2025 financial year.`]},"company:asmlol":{intro:`Modelled on ASML, a joint venture of the Dutch electronics giant Philips and the chip-equipment firm ASM International, set up to sell Philips' wafer steppers: machines that print chip patterns onto silicon with light. It began with 31 staff in a leaky wooden shed beside a Philips building in Eindhoven.`,how:`A stepper shines light through a mask carrying the chip's pattern and shrinks the image through lenses onto one small area of the wafer, then steps to the next spot. ASML designed and assembled the machines but bought its lenses from the German optics firm Zeiss and many other parts from outside suppliers.`,history:`ASML was founded in 1984. Its PAS 5500 of 1991 was the bestselling machine that made its name. In 2001 it bought the American lithography firm SVG and launched its TWINSCAN machines, which measure one wafer while printing another. In 2012 Intel, TSMC and Samsung invested in it to pay for extreme ultraviolet, or EUV, lithography, which uses light of 13.5 nanometres made by firing lasers at droplets of tin. The first EUV machines went into mass production at TSMC and Samsung in 2019. From 2023 the Dutch government restricted sales of its best machines to China.`,facts:[`ASML floated its shares in Amsterdam and New York (1995).`,`An EUV machine cost about $150 million (2019).`,`Its first High-NA EUV machine, priced around €350 million, went to Intel (2023).`,`ASML's sales were about €28 billion (2024).`]},"company:graphbore":{intro:`Modelled on Graphcore, the Bristol chip start-up founded by Nigel Toon and Simon Knowles, who had earlier built and sold the mobile chip firm Icera to NVIDIA. It designed the IPU, or intelligence processing unit, a processor built around the graphs of calculations inside machine-learning models.`,how:`The IPU spread more than a thousand small processor cores across one chip, each with its own memory beside it, so a whole model could sit on the chip rather than in slow external memory. Graphcore sold its chips in server boxes and wrote its own software, called Poplar, to run AI programs on them.`,history:`Toon and Knowles founded Graphcore in 2016. Its first IPU shipped in 2018, and in 2020 it launched a second-generation chip with 59 billion transistors and was valued at $2.8 billion. In 2022 its Bow IPU became the first processor made with TSMC's wafer-on-wafer stacking. But customers stuck with NVIDIA's chips and software, losses mounted and it cut staff in 2023. In July 2024 the Japanese group SoftBank bought it, reportedly for about $600 million.`,facts:[`Icera had been sold to NVIDIA for $367 million (2011).`,`The second IPU had 1,472 cores and 900 megabytes of memory on the chip (2020).`]},"company:mambonova":{intro:`Modelled on SambaNova Systems, the Palo Alto AI computing company founded by two Stanford professors, Kunle Olukotun and Christopher Ré, with Rodrigo Liang, who had run processor design at Sun and Oracle. It set out to build computers whose hardware reshapes itself to match the flow of data through an AI program.`,how:`SambaNova's chip, the Reconfigurable Dataflow Unit, is a grid of compute and memory units whose connections are set by its compiler for each model, so data streams from step to step without going back to memory. It sold whole racks of these machines, and AI services built on them, to companies and government laboratories.`,history:`SambaNova was founded in 2017, building on Olukotun's research into dataflow computers at Stanford. Its first DataScale systems shipped in 2020 to US government laboratories including Lawrence Livermore. In April 2021 SoftBank's Vision Fund led a $676 million round that valued it at more than $5 billion. In 2023 it launched the SN40L chip, with three tiers of memory to hold very large models, and in 2024 it opened a cloud service for fast chatbot answers.`,facts:[`Its funding round raised $676 million (2021).`,`The SN40L chip had about 102 billion transistors (2023).`]},"company:tenstorment":{intro:`Modelled on Tenstorrent, the Toronto AI chip company founded by Ljubisa Bajic, Milos Trajkovic and Ivan Hamer, engineers who had worked at AMD and NVIDIA. It set out to build processors that skip work a neural network does not need, scaling from one chip to huge clusters joined by ordinary networking.`,how:`Tenstorrent's chips are grids of small cores called Tensix, each pairing number-crunching units with simple general-purpose processors and memory of its own, so work can be split across many cores and many chips. It aimed to sell cards and servers far cheaper than the market leaders and to publish its software openly.`,history:`Bajic, Trajkovic and Hamer founded Tenstorrent in 2016. Its first chip, Grayskull, was ready in 2020. Jim Keller, the designer behind AMD's Zen and Apple's early iPhone chips, joined in 2021 and became chief executive in 2023. It built its designs around RISC-V, the free and open instruction set anyone may use, and in 2023 it began licensing its processor cores to firms such as LG. In December 2024 it raised $693 million from investors including Jeff Bezos and Samsung, at a valuation of about $2.6 billion.`,facts:[`Its funding round raised $693 million (2024).`,`Jim Keller took over as chief executive (2023).`]},"company:sketched":{intro:`Modelled on Etched, the chip start-up founded by the Harvard dropouts Gavin Uberti and Chris Zhu with Robert Wachen. They bet that transformers, the design behind chatbots, would dominate AI, so they would hard-wire that one design into silicon instead of building a flexible chip.`,how:`An ASIC, or application-specific integrated circuit, does one job only. By giving up the ability to run other kinds of neural network, Etched's chip can fill its area with the arithmetic transformers need, aiming for many times more throughput per chip than a graphics processor.`,history:`Uberti, Zhu and Wachen founded Etched in 2022, backed by Peter Thiel's fellowship for young founders, betting that the transformer design published by Google researchers in 2017 would not soon be replaced. In June 2024 it announced Sohu, its transformer-only chip to be made by TSMC, and raised $120 million. It claimed in 2024 that one server with eight Sohu chips could replace 160 of NVIDIA's H100 GPUs.`,facts:[`Sohu was designed for TSMC's 4-nanometre process (2024).`,`Etched's first big funding round raised $120 million (2024).`]},"company:dmattress":{intro:`Modelled on d-Matrix, the Santa Clara chip start-up founded by Sid Sheth and Sudeep Bhoja, who had built high-speed networking chips at Inphi. It set out to make AI inference, the work of running models that are already trained, cheaper by doing the arithmetic right where the numbers are stored.`,how:`In most chips, moving numbers between memory and the processor uses more energy than the sums themselves. d-Matrix builds digital in-memory compute, with multiply-and-add circuits woven into the memory arrays, so a model's weights never travel far. It aims to sell these chips on cards that plug into ordinary data-centre servers.`,history:`Sheth and Bhoja founded d-Matrix in 2019. In 2021 it showed its first test chip, Nighthawk. In September 2023 it raised $110 million from investors including Temasek and Microsoft's venture fund. In November 2024 it launched Corsair, its first product card, aimed at chatbots that need quick answers. In November 2025 it raised $275 million at a valuation of about $2 billion.`,facts:[`Its funding round raised $110 million (2023).`,`It was valued at about $2 billion (2025).`]},"company:qualcalm":{intro:`Modelled on Qualcomm, the San Diego company founded by Irwin Jacobs, Andrew Viterbi and five colleagues from Linkabit, a defence communications firm that Jacobs and Viterbi had also started. The name was short for quality communications, and it began by doing contract research for others.`,how:`Qualcomm earned its first money from contract work and a satellite messaging system for lorry fleets, and pinned its hopes on CDMA, a way of letting many phones share one radio channel by giving each call its own code, like people speaking different languages in one room. It planned to design the chips and license its patents to every phone maker using the technology.`,history:`Qualcomm was founded in July 1985. In 1989 it demonstrated CDMA for mobile phones, against the doubts of much of the industry. The first commercial CDMA networks opened in Hong Kong in 1995 and South Korea in 1996. In 1999 it sold its phone-making and network businesses to concentrate on chips and patent licences. It launched its Snapdragon phone processors in 2007. In 2019 it settled a worldwide patent war with Apple and announced the Cloud AI 100, a chip for running AI in data centres.`,facts:[`Its share price rose about 2,600% in a single year (1999).`,`Apple paid Qualcomm about $4.5 billion to settle their dispute (2019).`,`It announced its AI200 and AI250 chips for AI data centres (2025).`]},"company:byren":{intro:`Modelled on Biren Technology, the Shanghai graphics and AI chip company founded by Zhang Wen, a former president of the AI firm SenseTime, with a team of experienced chip engineers. It set out to build general-purpose GPUs, the kind of chip used to train AI, so that China would not depend on American suppliers.`,how:`Biren designed its chips and paid foundries to make them, selling cards and servers to Chinese cloud companies, AI labs and state-backed data centres. It wrote its own software, called BIRENSUPA, so that AI programs written for NVIDIA's hardware could be moved across.`,history:`Biren was founded in 2019. By 2021 it had raised billions of yuan from Chinese investors. In August 2022 it unveiled the BR100, a chip with 77 billion transistors made by TSMC that it said rivalled NVIDIA's best. US export rules of October 2022 limited TSMC's ability to make it, and in October 2023 the US put Biren on its Entity List. On 2 January 2026 it listed on the Hong Kong stock exchange, and its shares closed 76% above their offer price on the first day.`,facts:[`The BR100 claimed about 1,000 trillion 16-bit calculations a second (2022).`,`Its Hong Kong share sale raised about HK$5.58 billion (2026).`]},"company:cambricorn":{intro:`Modelled on Cambricon Technologies, the Beijing AI chip company founded by the brothers Chen Tianshi and Chen Yunji, researchers at the Chinese Academy of Sciences' Institute of Computing Technology. Their academic work had produced some of the first processor designs made specially for neural networks, and the company set out to turn it into products.`,how:`Cambricon licensed its neural processing unit, a ready-made block of chip design for running AI, to the makers of phone chips, who built it into their own processors. It planned server chips of its own for Chinese cloud companies, internet firms and state data centres.`,history:`The Chen brothers founded Cambricon in 2016. In 2017 its Cambricon-1A design became the AI engine of Huawei's Kirin 970 phone chip. In 2018 it launched its first server chip, the Siyuan MLU100, and in 2019 Huawei switched to an AI design of its own. Cambricon listed on Shanghai's new STAR Market for technology firms in July 2020. The US put it on its Entity List in December 2022, cutting it off from TSMC. As American export rules kept NVIDIA's best chips out of China, its sales soared and it made its first full-year profit in 2025.`,facts:[`Its sales were about 2.9 billion yuan in the first half of 2025.`,`The Kirin 970 was among the first phone chips with an AI engine (2017).`]},"company:openrooter":{intro:`Modelled on OpenRouter, the company that offers a single online gateway to hundreds of AI language models from dozens of providers. It was founded by Alex Atallah, a co-founder of the NFT marketplace OpenSea, and Louis Vichy, as developers faced a bewildering choice of models, prices and providers.`,how:`Developers send their requests to OpenRouter's one API, and it routes each to a provider offering the chosen model, switching to another if one is down or slow. Customers buy credits, paying the providers' prices plus a small fee, and OpenRouter publishes rankings of which models people actually use.`,history:`Atallah and Vichy launched OpenRouter in 2023. By 2024 it offered hundreds of models, and its public rankings of the most-used models became a widely watched guide to the market. In June 2025 it announced it had raised $40 million from Andreessen Horowitz and Menlo Ventures, valuing it at about $500 million. In December 2025 it published a study with Andreessen Horowitz of 100 trillion tokens of real use on its service.`,facts:[`OpenRouter was valued at about $500 million (2025).`,`Its study covered 100 trillion tokens of traffic (2025).`]},"company:clowdera":{intro:`Modelled on Cloudera, the Palo Alto start-up founded by engineers from Google, Yahoo, Facebook and Oracle to sell Hadoop, free software for storing and crunching huge amounts of data across clusters of cheap servers. It aimed to do for Hadoop what Red Hat had done for Linux.`,how:`Hadoop splits files across many machines and processes them where they sit, using the MapReduce method Google had described. Cloudera packaged it into a tested distribution, added management tools and sold support subscriptions and training to banks, retailers and telecoms firms that wanted big data without building it themselves.`,history:`Christophe Bisciglia, Amr Awadallah, Jeff Hammerbacher and Mike Olson founded Cloudera on 27 June 2008. Hadoop's creator Doug Cutting joined in 2009. In 2014 Intel invested $740 million for a stake of about 18%. It floated in April 2017, just as cloud services such as Amazon's were drawing customers away. In January 2019 it merged with its rival Hortonworks, and in 2021 the investment firms KKR and Clayton, Dubilier and Rice took it private for about $5.3 billion.`,facts:[`Intel's investment valued Cloudera at about $4.1 billion (2014).`,`Its shares were first sold to the public at $15 each (2017).`]},"company:hortonwho":{intro:`Modelled on Hortonworks, the company Yahoo spun out with its own Hadoop engineers, the team that had written much of the free big-data software. It was named after Horton, the elephant in a Dr Seuss book, since Hadoop's mascot was a toy elephant, and it promised to keep every line of its product open source.`,how:`Hortonworks gave away its Hadoop distribution entirely free and made money from support subscriptions, training and consulting, betting that companies would pay the experts who had built the software. It contributed its improvements back to the Apache Software Foundation's projects rather than keeping closed add-ons.`,history:`Hortonworks was founded in June 2011 with $23 million from Yahoo and the venture firm Benchmark Capital, led by Eric Baldeschwieler. Its Hortonworks Data Platform shipped in 2012, and partners such as Microsoft built cloud services on it. It floated on the Nasdaq exchange in December 2014. Losses continued as customers moved to cloud services, and in January 2019 it merged into its rival Cloudera.`,facts:[`Yahoo and Benchmark put in $23 million to start it (2011).`,`Its shares were first sold to the public at $16 each (2014).`]},"company:mapper":{intro:`Modelled on MapR Technologies, the San Jose start-up founded by John Schroeder and M C Srivas, a former Google search engineer, to sell a faster, sturdier version of Hadoop. Instead of Hadoop's own file system, written in Java, MapR wrote a new one in C and C++.`,how:`MapR's file system could be read and written like an ordinary network drive and could copy snapshots between data centres, which plain Hadoop could not. It sold its distribution, with this closed core, under licences and subscriptions, competing on speed and reliability with Cloudera and Hortonworks.`,history:`Schroeder and Srivas founded MapR in 2009. It shipped its first distribution in 2011. In 2012 Amazon offered it on its Elastic MapReduce cloud service. In 2014 Google Capital led a $110 million investment in the company. In May 2019 it warned it would close and lay off more than 100 staff unless it found a buyer. In August 2019 Hewlett Packard Enterprise bought its technology and staff.`,facts:[`Google Capital led a $110 million funding round (2014).`,`It warned of more than 100 job losses (2019).`]},"company:databrix":{intro:`Modelled on Databricks, the San Francisco company founded by the creators of Apache Spark at the University of California, Berkeley's AMPLab, including Ali Ghodsi, Matei Zaharia and Ion Stoica. Spark processed big data in memory, far faster than Hadoop's MapReduce, and the company was set up to sell it as a cloud service.`,how:`Databricks runs Spark for its customers on rented cloud computers, with shared notebooks where data teams write code, query tables and train machine-learning models together. Customers pay for the computing they use, and the free open-source projects it backs draw in users who may become paying customers.`,history:`Seven Berkeley researchers founded Databricks in 2013. Spark became a top-level Apache project in 2014. In 2019 it released Delta Lake as open source, and in 2020 it named its idea of a lakehouse: a cheap data lake with the reliability of a database warehouse. In June 2023 it bought the AI start-up MosaicML for $1.3 billion, and in June 2024 the table-format company Tabular. In December 2024 it raised $10 billion at a $62 billion valuation, and by December 2025 it was valued at $134 billion.`,facts:[`Spark set a world record by sorting 100 terabytes in 23 minutes (2014).`,`Its revenue was about $1.6 billion in its 2023 financial year.`,`It was valued at $134 billion (2025).`]},"company:confusent":{intro:`Modelled on Confluent, the Mountain View company founded by Jay Kreps, Neha Narkhede and Jun Rao, who had created Apache Kafka at the business network LinkedIn. Kafka passed streams of events, such as clicks, payments and sensor readings, between a company's systems as they happened, instead of in nightly batches.`,how:`Kafka stores events in an append-only log that many programs can read at their own pace, and it spreads the log across many servers so that no data is lost. Confluent sold support, training and extra tools to companies running Kafka on their own servers.`,history:`Kreps built Kafka at LinkedIn from 2010, naming it after the writer Franz Kafka, and it was released as open source in 2011. He, Narkhede and Rao founded Confluent in 2014. It launched Confluent Cloud, which ran Kafka for customers, in 2017. It floated on the Nasdaq exchange in June 2021. Its revenue reached $964 million in 2024. IBM agreed to buy it in December 2025 for about $11 billion and completed the deal on 17 March 2026.`,facts:[`IBM paid $31 a share in cash (2026).`,`Confluent counted more than 6,500 customers (2026).`]},"company:splunky":{intro:`Modelled on Splunk, the San Francisco company founded by Michael Baum, Rob Das and Erik Swan to search machine data: the endless logs that servers, networks and applications write about what they are doing. Its name came from spelunking, the hobby of exploring caves.`,how:`Splunk collected log files from across a company's computers, indexed every line and let engineers search them like a web search engine, to find the cause of an outage or a break-in. It sold licences priced by how much data customers fed in each day, so its bills grew with their data.`,history:`Baum, Das and Swan founded Splunk in 2003. It floated in April 2012 and grew into a leading tool for security teams hunting attackers. In 2019 it began switching customers from permanent licences to subscriptions and its cloud service. Its revenue reached $3.65 billion in its 2023 financial year. Cisco agreed to buy it in September 2023 and completed the $28 billion deal on 18 March 2024, the largest purchase in Cisco's history.`,facts:[`Cisco paid $157 a share in cash (2024).`,`Splunk's revenue was $3.65 billion in its 2023 financial year.`]},"company:elastick":{intro:`Modelled on Elastic, the company founded in Amsterdam around Elasticsearch, the open-source search engine that the developer Shay Banon wrote after building a search tool for his wife's recipes. Elasticsearch let any website or company search vast amounts of text in a fraction of a second.`,how:`Elasticsearch spreads its index across many servers and answers searches sent through a simple web interface, so it grows by adding machines. Elastic gave the core away free and earned money from support, training and extra features for large customers.`,history:`Banon released Elasticsearch in 2010 and founded the company with Steven Schuurman, Uri Boness and Simon Willnauer in February 2012. In 2013 the Logstash and Kibana projects joined it, forming the ELK stack for collecting, searching and charting logs. It floated in New York in 2018. In January 2021 it moved Elasticsearch off its open-source licence to stop Amazon selling it as a cloud service, and Amazon answered by copying the code into its own OpenSearch project. In August 2024 it added the open-source AGPL licence again, and its revenue reached $1.483 billion in its 2025 financial year.`,facts:[`Its shares nearly doubled on their first day of trading (2018).`,`Its revenue was $1.483 billion in its 2025 financial year.`]},"company:datadawg":{intro:`Modelled on Datadog, the New York company founded by Olivier Pomel and Alexis Lê-Quôc, two French engineers who had worked together at the education firm Wireless Generation. They had watched developers and operations staff blame each other for outages and wanted to give both one shared view of how servers and apps were performing.`,how:`Datadog's small agent program runs on each server, sending measurements such as processor load and error rates to Datadog's cloud, where teams watch live dashboards and get alerts. Customers pay a subscription for each server watched, so spending grows as their systems grow.`,history:`Pomel and Lê-Quôc founded Datadog in 2010, as cloud computing was taking off. It launched its monitoring service in 2012. It added application performance monitoring in 2017 and log management in 2018. It floated on the Nasdaq exchange in September 2019. Its revenue grew from $363 million in 2019 to $2.68 billion in 2024, by which time it employed about 6,500 people.`,facts:[`Its shares rose about 39% on their first day of trading (2019).`,`Its revenue was $2.68 billion (2024).`]},"company:newrelish":{intro:`Modelled on New Relic, the San Francisco company founded by Lew Cirne, whose company name is an anagram of his own. Cirne had already built and sold Wily Technology, an early maker of tools for watching how business software performed, and set out to offer the same insight as a simple web service.`,how:`Developers add New Relic's small agent to their application, which reports how long each page, database query and outside call takes. The data goes to New Relic's servers, where dashboards show the slow spots and errors, and customers pay a monthly subscription instead of buying and installing monitoring software.`,history:`Cirne founded New Relic in 2008, two years after selling Wily to Computer Associates in 2006. It began by monitoring web apps written in the Ruby on Rails framework in 2008 and soon added Java and other languages. It floated on the New York Stock Exchange on 12 December 2014. In November 2023 the investment firms Francisco Partners and TPG took it private for about $6.5 billion.`,facts:[`Computer Associates paid $375 million for Wily Technology (2006).`,`New Relic was taken private for about $6.5 billion (2023).`]},"company:dynatrice":{intro:`Modelled on Dynatrace, founded in Linz, Austria, as dynaTrace software by Bernd Greifeneder and Sok-Kheng Taing to trace every transaction through complicated business applications. Its tools followed a single request as it hopped from web server to application to database, showing exactly where time was lost.`,how:`Dynatrace's agent watches applications from the inside, recording each transaction's path, and its software adds up thousands of these traces to spot slowdowns and errors. It sold licences and support to large companies, such as banks and retailers, whose websites could not afford to be slow.`,history:`Greifeneder and Taing founded dynaTrace in Linz in 2005. In 2011 the American software firm Compuware bought it. In 2014 the investment firm Thoma Bravo took Compuware private and made Dynatrace a separate company. In 2016 it launched a rebuilt platform with a single agent that found and mapped all of a system's parts by itself, and in 2017 it added Davis, an AI engine that pinpoints the root cause of problems. It floated on the New York Stock Exchange in August 2019, raising about $570 million.`,facts:[`Compuware paid about $256 million for dynaTrace (2011).`,`Its revenue was about $1.7 billion in its 2025 financial year.`]},"company:dockr":{intro:`Modelled on Docker Inc, which began as dotCloud, a start-up founded in Paris by Solomon Hykes and Sebastien Pahl and moved to San Francisco through the Y Combinator start-up school. dotCloud ran customers' web apps for them on its own servers, using Linux tricks to keep each app in its own sealed box.`,how:`dotCloud packaged each customer's application with everything it needed and ran many such packages side by side on one server, kept apart by features of the Linux kernel, so the apps never clashed. Customers paid to have their code hosted and scaled up without managing servers themselves.`,history:`Hykes started dotCloud in Paris in 2008 and moved it to the United States in 2010. In March 2013 he showed its internal tool, Docker, at the PyCon conference and released it as open source, and containers caught on so fast that the company renamed itself Docker that October and sold the dotCloud hosting business in 2014. It was valued at over $1 billion in 2015 but struggled to sell products to big companies as Google's Kubernetes took over the running of containers. In November 2019 it sold its enterprise business to Mirantis, and from 2021 it charged larger companies for its Docker Desktop app.`,facts:[`Docker was valued at about $1 billion (2015).`,`Docker Desktop became paid for firms with more than 250 staff (2021).`]},"company:redhot":{intro:`Modelled on Red Hat, the Linux company formed by Marc Ewing, who named his software after the red Cornell lacrosse cap he wore at university, and Bob Young, who sold Linux books and discs by mail order. It packaged the free Linux operating system so that it was easier to install and use.`,how:`Linux and most of the programs around it were free, so Red Hat could not charge for the code. It sold boxed copies with manuals and, above all, subscriptions for support, updates and guarantees, betting that businesses would pay a trusted supplier to stand behind free software.`,history:`Marc Ewing began building his own Linux distribution in 1993, naming it after his red hat. He released Red Hat Linux in 1994, and in 1995 his business joined Bob Young's to form Red Hat Software, based in North Carolina. Its share sale in August 1999 was one of the biggest first-day jumps of the dot-com boom. In 2002 it launched Red Hat Enterprise Linux, sold only by subscription. In 2012 it became the first open-source company to take $1 billion in a year. IBM bought it for $34 billion in July 2019, gaining its OpenShift container platform and Ansible automation tools.`,facts:[`Red Hat's revenue was $3.4 billion in its 2019 financial year.`,`IBM paid $190 a share, its biggest purchase ever (2019).`]},"company:vmwhere":{intro:`Modelled on VMware, the Palo Alto company founded by Diane Greene, her husband Mendel Rosenblum, a Stanford professor, and three others to bring virtual machines to ordinary PCs. A virtual machine is a whole computer imitated in software, so one real machine can run several operating systems at once.`,how:`VMware's software sits between the hardware and the operating systems, catching the instructions that would clash and handling them itself, a hard trick on Intel's x86 chips. It sold licences first to developers who wanted to test programs on several systems, and planned versions that would let companies pack many virtual servers onto one box.`,history:`Greene, Rosenblum and their partners founded VMware in 1998. It released VMware Workstation in May 1999. Its ESX Server of 2001 let data centres run many virtual servers on one machine, sharply cutting hardware bills. The storage firm EMC bought it in 2004. Dell took over EMC, and with it VMware, in 2016. In November 2023 Broadcom bought VMware for $69 billion and moved customers to bundled subscriptions, raising prices steeply for many.`,facts:[`EMC paid $625 million for VMware (2004).`,`Broadcom paid about $69 billion (2023).`]},"company:pivotail":{intro:`Modelled on Pivotal Software, the San Francisco company carved out of the storage firm EMC and its offshoot VMware, with an investment from General Electric. Led by Paul Maritz, a former Microsoft executive and VMware chief, it gathered their cloud software, data tools and an agile programming consultancy into one business.`,how:`Pivotal's main product, Cloud Foundry, let a company's developers push their code and have it run automatically on the company's own servers or a cloud, without asking anyone for machines. It also sold the Greenplum data warehouse and backed the Spring framework for Java programs, while Pivotal Labs taught clients to write software in small, fast steps.`,history:`Pivotal was launched in April 2013 with $105 million from General Electric. In 2015 it handed Cloud Foundry to a new independent foundation to attract other backers. It floated on the New York Stock Exchange in April 2018 at $15 a share, but its shares sank as Kubernetes became the favourite way of running cloud software. In December 2019 VMware bought it back for about $2.7 billion and folded it into its Tanzu products.`,facts:[`General Electric paid $105 million for a 10% stake (2013).`,`VMware paid about $2.7 billion to take it back (2019).`]},"company:coreouch":{intro:`Modelled on CoreOS, the San Francisco start-up founded by Alex Polvi and Brandon Philips to rebuild Linux for huge fleets of servers. Its slimmed-down operating system did little more than run containers, and it updated itself automatically, the way the Chrome browser does.`,how:`CoreOS Linux had no package manager: applications ran in containers, and the system swapped in a whole new version at each update and rolled back if it failed. The company wrote etcd, a small database that keeps a cluster of servers agreed on its settings, and planned to sell support and tools for running big clusters.`,history:`Polvi and Philips founded CoreOS in 2013 and released CoreOS Linux and etcd that year. In December 2014 it launched rkt, a rival to Docker's container engine, arguing that Docker had grown too complicated. In 2015 it launched Tectonic, a commercial edition of Google's Kubernetes container manager, which stored its own state in etcd. In January 2018 Red Hat bought it for about $250 million. In 2020 Container Linux was retired in favour of Fedora CoreOS.`,facts:[`Red Hat paid about $250 million for CoreOS (2018).`,`Its etcd database was donated to the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (2018).`]},"company:mesmersphere":{intro:`Modelled on Mesosphere, the San Francisco start-up founded by Florian Leibert, Tobias Knaup and Ben Hindman around Apache Mesos, software from the University of California, Berkeley, that treats a whole data centre's servers as one big computer. Leibert had used Mesos at Twitter, which ran its services on it.`,how:`Mesos pools the processors and memory of thousands of machines and hands out slices to applications as they need them, so servers are not left idle. Mesosphere wrote Marathon, a scheduler that keeps long-running services alive across the cluster, and sold support and tools to companies running Mesos.`,history:`Mesos began as a research project at Berkeley in 2009, and Leibert, Knaup and Hindman founded Mesosphere in 2013. In 2015 it launched DC/OS, short for Data Center Operating System, and in April 2016 it released it as open source with backing from Microsoft and others. Kubernetes won the contest to run containers, and in August 2019 the company renamed itself D2iQ and moved to Kubernetes. In December 2023 D2iQ wound down, and Nutanix took over some of its technology and staff.`,facts:[`Apple ran its Siri services on Mesos (2015).`,`Microsoft and Hewlett Packard Enterprise invested in Mesosphere (2016).`]},"company:rancid":{intro:`Modelled on Rancher Labs, the Cupertino start-up founded by Sheng Liang, Shannon Williams, Darren Shepherd and Will Chan, who had earlier built and sold the cloud software company Cloud.com. It set out to make containers easy for ordinary companies to run, on any servers or cloud.`,how:`Rancher's open-source software gave a company one control panel for many clusters of container servers, wherever they ran, with shared security and user accounts. It gave the software away free and sold support subscriptions to large businesses.`,history:`Liang and his partners founded Rancher in 2014. It released Rancher 1.0 in 2016. As Kubernetes took over, it rebuilt the product in 2018 to manage many Kubernetes clusters at once. In February 2019 it released K3s, a cut-down Kubernetes small enough for shop tills, factory gateways and other edge devices. The German Linux company SUSE agreed to buy it in July 2020 and completed the deal on 1 December 2020.`,facts:[`Citrix had bought Cloud.com for about $200 million (2011).`,`More than 30,000 teams used Rancher's software (2020).`]},"company:hashicorpse":{intro:`Modelled on HashiCorp, the San Francisco company founded by Mitchell Hashimoto and Armon Dadgar, who met at the University of Washington. Hashimoto had written Vagrant, a free tool that builds identical virtual development machines with one command, and the pair set out to automate every step of running software in data centres and clouds.`,how:`HashiCorp released its tools as open source so that developers could adopt them freely, then sold enterprise versions with extra features and support to big companies. Its plan was a family of tools, each tackling one job in building and running software.`,history:`Hashimoto released Vagrant in 2010, and he and Dadgar founded HashiCorp in 2012. Terraform, launched in July 2014, let engineers describe their servers and networks in code and build them on any cloud, and Vault of 2015 kept passwords and keys safe. It floated on the Nasdaq exchange in December 2021, valued at about $13 billion. In August 2023 it moved its tools to the Business Source License, which bars rivals from selling them, and supporters answered by forking Terraform as OpenTofu. IBM bought it for $6.4 billion in February 2025.`,facts:[`HashiCorp's market value was about $13 billion on its first day of trading (2021).`,`IBM paid $35 a share in cash (2025).`]},"company:windyriver":{intro:`Modelled on Wind River Systems, which began as a consultancy run by Jerry Fiddler in the San Francisco Bay Area, writing software for machines such as film and video equipment. Fiddler and David Wilner named it after Wind River in Wyoming, where Fiddler had been on holiday, and specialised in embedded systems: computers hidden inside other machines.`,how:`Embedded computers must react to events within strict time limits, so Wind River wrote real-time software that guaranteed how quickly it would respond. It sold development tools and operating system licences to makers of telecoms gear, aircraft, factory robots and vehicles, often charging a royalty on every device shipped.`,history:`Fiddler began consulting in 1981. In 1983 he and Wilner incorporated Wind River with $3,000 each. In 1987 it launched VxWorks, a real-time operating system that became its main product. In 1997 VxWorks ran NASA's Mars Pathfinder lander, whose computer kept resetting itself until engineers on Earth found a timing bug and fixed it by radio. Intel bought Wind River for about $884 million in 2009. After a spell with the investment firm TPG from 2018, it was bought by the car-parts maker Aptiv for $4.3 billion in 2022.`,facts:[`VxWorks also ran NASA's Spirit and Opportunity rovers on Mars (2004).`,`Aptiv paid $4.3 billion for Wind River (2022).`]},"company:qnix":{intro:`Modelled on QNX Software Systems, founded in Ottawa as Quantum Software Systems by Gordon Bell and Dan Dodge, two University of Waterloo graduates. They wrote a real-time operating system for the new microcomputers, built around a tiny core that did little more than pass messages between programs.`,how:`In a microkernel system like QNX, the drivers, file systems and networking run as separate programs outside the core, so if one crashes it can be restarted while the rest keeps running. That made QNX attractive for machines that must never stop, such as medical equipment, factory controllers and telephone systems, and makers paid a licence for each device.`,history:`Bell and Dodge founded the company on 30 March 1980 and first called their system QUNIX, a play on Unix, changing the name to QNX after AT&T objected. The car electronics firm Harman bought QNX in 2004. Research In Motion, the BlackBerry maker, bought it in April 2010. It was the base of RIM's PlayBook tablet in 2011 and its BlackBerry 10 phones in 2013. By 2019 QNX ran in more than 150 million vehicles.`,facts:[`RIM paid about $200 million for QNX (2010).`,`QNX software ran in about 235 million vehicles (2024).`]},"company:greenerhills":{intro:`Modelled on Green Hills Software, the Santa Barbara company founded by Dan O'Dowd and Carl Rosenberg to make compilers: programs that turn code written by people into instructions a processor can run. It focused on embedded processors, the chips hidden inside other equipment.`,how:`Green Hills sold optimising compilers, debuggers and development tools that produced fast, compact code for many kinds of embedded chip. It aimed at customers who could not tolerate failures, such as defence, aircraft and medical companies, and charged high prices for software built and tested to strict standards.`,history:`O'Dowd and Rosenberg founded Green Hills in 1982. In 1997 it launched Integrity, a real-time operating system that walls programs off from each other so that one fault cannot spread. In November 2008 Integrity-178B became the first operating system certified by the US National Security Agency's testing scheme to the high EAL 6+ security level. In 2022 O'Dowd ran for the US Senate in California with a campaign attacking Tesla's Full Self-Driving software as unsafe.`,facts:[`Integrity-178B was certified to EAL 6+, then the highest rating given to an operating system (2008).`,`O'Dowd's Senate campaign was run almost entirely as adverts against Tesla (2022).`]},"company:bosh":{intro:`Modelled on Robert Bosch GmbH, the Stuttgart engineering company founded by Robert Bosch as a workshop for precision mechanics and electrical engineering. It made its name with magneto ignitions for car engines, then added spark plugs, headlamps, horns and diesel fuel-injection pumps, becoming the main supplier of electrical parts to Germany's car makers.`,how:`Bosch made parts and systems that car makers built into their vehicles, and sold power tools, radios and household appliances under its own name. Robert Bosch was known for paying well and for bringing in an eight-hour working day early, and the firm put quality and long-term growth ahead of quick profits.`,history:`Robert Bosch opened his Stuttgart workshop in November 1886; in 1902 its engineer Gottlob Honold produced a high-voltage magneto ignition with a spark plug, and in 1927 it began making diesel injection pumps. In 1964 most of the company passed to the Robert Bosch Foundation, a charity, as its founder had wished. With Mercedes-Benz it brought anti-lock brakes, ABS, to a production car in 1978, and in 1979 it launched Motronic, which controlled an engine's fuel and ignition with one computer. In 1986 it presented the CAN bus, a network that lets a car's computers talk over shared wires. In 2003 it helped found AUTOSAR, a shared standard for the software in car control units. In 2019 German prosecutors fined it €90 million over its part in the diesel emissions scandal.`,facts:[`Bosch employed about 418,000 people (2024).`,`Its sales were about €90 billion (2024).`]},"company:victor":{intro:`Modelled on Vector Informatik, the Stuttgart software company founded by Eberhard Hinderer, Martin Litschel and Helmut Schelling, engineers who had worked on car electronics. It set out to build tools for developing and testing the networks that were starting to link the electronic control units inside cars.`,how:`Vector sells software and hardware interfaces that let engineers watch, record and simulate the messages flying between a car's control units, so they can test one unit before the rest of the car exists. It sold its tools to car makers and their suppliers, growing with every new network in the car, and stayed privately owned.`,history:`Vector was founded on 1 April 1988. In 1992 it launched CANalyzer, a tool for watching and analysing traffic on Bosch's CAN bus, and in the 1990s it added CANoe, which could simulate a whole network of control units. In the 2000s it began selling MICROSAR, its basic software for control units built to the AUTOSAR standard. By the 2020s it employed thousands of people in offices around the world.`,facts:[`CANalyzer arrived six years after Bosch first presented the CAN bus (1992).`,`The AUTOSAR standard that Vector's MICROSAR follows was founded (2003).`]},"company:elektrobot":{intro:`Modelled on Elektrobit Automotive, which grew from 3SOFT, a software company founded in Erlangen, Germany, to write programs for embedded computers. It specialised in the software inside cars' electronic control units and the tools used to configure it.`,how:`Elektrobit sold ready-made basic software for car control units, configuration tools, and software for navigation and dashboard displays, licensing it to car makers and their suppliers, who built it into their vehicles. Using shared basic software meant car makers did not have to write the same plumbing again for every unit.`,history:`3SOFT was founded in Erlangen in 1988. In 2004 the Finnish technology group Elektrobit bought it, and the business took the Elektrobit name. In the 2000s its EB tresos tools and software for the AUTOSAR standard, founded by car makers and suppliers in 2003, became widely used. In 2015 the German car-parts giant Continental bought Elektrobit Automotive for about €600 million.`,facts:[`Continental paid about €600 million for Elektrobit Automotive (2015).`,`AUTOSAR was set up by firms including BMW, Bosch, Continental and Volkswagen (2003).`]},"company:cariod":{intro:`Modelled on CARIAD, the software company Volkswagen set up to write one operating system and cloud for all its brands, from Volkswagen and Audi to Porsche. Its name stands for Car, I Am Digital, and it aimed to make the group less dependent on suppliers and able to update its cars over the air, as Tesla did.`,how:`CARIAD gathered thousands of software engineers from across the group to build a shared platform for infotainment, driver assistance and the car's central computers, which every brand would use to save cost. It billed the group's brands for its work, so its losses were really part of the cost of building the cars.`,history:`Volkswagen founded the unit as Car.Software.Org in 2020. It was renamed CARIAD in 2021. In 2022 its software problems delayed the electric Porsche Macan and Audi Q6 e-tron, and chief executive Herbert Diess left Volkswagen that year partly over the troubles. CARIAD lost €2.1 billion in 2022 and €2.4 billion in 2023. In June 2024 Volkswagen agreed to invest up to $5 billion in a joint venture with the American electric car maker Rivian, which would supply the software for its future cars.`,facts:[`CARIAD lost €2.4 billion (2023).`,`Volkswagen raised its Rivian commitment to as much as $5.8 billion (2024).`]},"company:shockingly":{intro:`Modelled on Shockley Semiconductor Laboratory, set up in Mountain View, California, by William Shockley, co-inventor of the transistor at Bell Labs, with money from Arnold Beckman's scientific instrument company. It was the first firm to make silicon semiconductor devices in the orchards of the Santa Clara Valley, near where Shockley had grown up.`,how:`Shockley hired brilliant young physicists and engineers to make transistors and other devices from silicon, which copes with heat better than germanium. He steered their work toward his four-layer diode, a tricky switching device, and managed his staff badly: he was suspicious, domineering and quick to belittle them.`,history:`Shockley shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1956, the year he opened the laboratory with Beckman's backing. In September 1957 eight of his best staff, whom he called the traitorous eight, left together to found Fairchild Semiconductor. By 1960 the four-layer diode had found few buyers, and Beckman sold the laboratory to the Clevite company that year. It passed to ITT in 1965 and closed in 1968.`,facts:[`Shockley shared the Nobel Prize with John Bardeen and Walter Brattain (1956).`,`Eight of his staff resigned on the same day (1957).`]},"company:fairychild":{intro:`Modelled on Fairchild Semiconductor, founded in Mountain View, California, by eight young scientists who walked out of Shockley's laboratory, among them Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore. Their money came from Fairchild Camera and Instrument, owned by the inventor and investor Sherman Fairchild, which kept the right to buy them out.`,how:`Fairchild made silicon transistors, at first for military and aerospace buyers who paid high prices for parts that worked reliably in missiles and computers. Its team pushed new ways of making many transistors at once on a single wafer of silicon, cutting the cost of each.`,history:`The eight founded Fairchild in September 1957. In 1959 Jean Hoerni invented the planar process, which protects transistors under a layer of glassy oxide, and Noyce used it to design an integrated circuit with all its parts and wiring on one chip. Its first working planar chips were made in 1960 and sold as Micrologic from 1961. In 1965 Moore, its research director, predicted that the number of parts on a chip would double every year. Noyce and Moore left in 1968 to found Intel. The French oil-services firm Schlumberger bought Fairchild in 1979.`,facts:[`Each of the eight founders put in $500 of his own money (1957).`,`Fairchild Camera put up about $1.4 million to start the company (1957).`,`IBM bought 100 of its first transistors at $150 each (1958).`,`ON Semiconductor bought Fairchild for about $2.4 billion (2016).`]},"company:taxesinst":{intro:`Modelled on Texas Instruments of Dallas, which grew out of Geophysical Service, a company that searched for oil with sound waves and built military electronics in the Second World War. Renamed for its growing electronics business, it bought a licence to make transistors from Western Electric and set out to make them better and cheaper than anyone.`,how:`TI made electronic parts and systems for the military and for other manufacturers, and it was willing to bet on mass markets for new devices, cutting prices to sell in huge volumes. Its research laboratory hired leading scientists to find better materials and cheaper processes.`,history:`Geophysical Service was founded in 1930 and renamed Texas Instruments in 1951. In 1954 its chemist Gordon Teal made the first commercial silicon transistors, and that year it supplied the transistors for the Regency TR-1, the first pocket transistor radio. In September 1958 a new engineer, Jack Kilby, built the first integrated circuit, a sliver of germanium carrying several parts. In 1967 its engineers built a prototype handheld calculator, the Cal Tech, to show what chips could do. Its TI-99/4A home computer lost hundreds of millions of dollars before TI quit the home computer market in 1983. It bought National Semiconductor in 2011 to strengthen its analogue chip business.`,facts:[`The Regency TR-1 radio cost $49.95 (1954).`,`Its Speak and Spell toy talked using a single speech chip (1978).`,`Kilby won the Nobel Prize in Physics for the integrated circuit (2000).`,`TI paid about $6.5 billion for National Semiconductor (2011).`]},"company:rational":{intro:`Modelled on National Semiconductor, founded in Danbury, Connecticut, by Bernard Rothlein and a group of engineers who left the Sperry Rand company to make transistors. It was one of a wave of small firms set up to make semiconductor devices for the military and computer makers.`,how:`National made transistors and, as chips arrived, standard integrated circuits sold in large volumes to equipment makers. It competed on cost, running its factories hard and pricing aggressively to win orders from bigger rivals.`,history:`National Semiconductor was founded in Danbury in 1959. In 1967 Charlie Sporck, Fairchild's tough production chief, left to run it, bringing several colleagues, and moved its headquarters to Santa Clara, California. Under Sporck it grew into one of the largest chipmakers of the 1970s on cheap logic and analogue chips. It bought its old rival Fairchild Semiconductor in 1987. In 1997 it bought the processor designer Cyrix and spun Fairchild off again. Texas Instruments bought National for $6.5 billion in 2011, mainly for its analogue chips that manage power.`,facts:[`Charlie Sporck ran National from 1967 to 1991.`,`Texas Instruments paid about $6.5 billion for National (2011).`]},"company:mostlytek":{intro:`Modelled on Mostek, the chip company based in Carrollton, Texas, founded by L J Sevin and other engineers from Texas Instruments, with backing from the electronics firm Sprague Electric. It made MOS chips, a newer kind of chip that packed transistors densely and suited memories and calculators.`,how:`Mostek designed and made its own MOS chips, using ion implantation, a way of shooting atoms into silicon with precision, to make them faster and more reliable. It sold memory chips and calculator chips in large volumes to computer and calculator makers.`,history:`Mostek was founded in 1969. In 1971 it made one of the first calculators on a single chip, the MK6010, for the Japanese firm Busicom. Its MK4096 of 1973 was a 4-kilobit DRAM in a small 16-pin package, made possible by sending each address in two halves, a trick the whole industry copied. Its 16-kilobit MK4116 of 1976 became the industry's bestselling memory chip. United Technologies bought Mostek for about $345 million in 1979. Undercut by Japanese makers, it was closed in 1985.`,facts:[`The MK4116 held 16,384 bits (1976).`,`United Technologies paid about $345 million for Mostek (1979).`]},"company:mosstech":{intro:`Modelled on MOS Technology, a chipmaker in Norristown, Pennsylvania, near Philadelphia, set up by managers from General Instrument with backing from the industrial firm Allen-Bradley. It made MOS chips, above all for the booming market in electronic calculators.`,how:`MOS Technology designed chips and made them in its own small factory, competing on price for orders from calculator makers and other electronics firms. As a small company, it had to win business by being cheaper and quicker than the big chipmakers.`,history:`MOS Technology was founded in 1969 and made calculator chips for firms such as Texas Instruments. In 1974 Chuck Peddle and a group of engineers who had worked on Motorola's 6800 processor joined it, and in September 1975 they launched the 6502, sold for $25 when Intel's and Motorola's processors cost well over $150. Motorola sued over the design, and the calculator maker Commodore bought the struggling firm in 1976. The 6502 powered the Apple II and Commodore PET of 1977, and a cut-down version ran the Atari 2600 console that year. Versions of it also ran the BBC Micro of 1981 and Nintendo's Famicom of 1983.`,facts:[`The 6502 cost $25 at its launch (1975).`,`The 6502 had about 3,500 transistors (1975).`]},"company:zilag":{intro:`Modelled on Zilog, the chip company founded by Federico Faggin, who had led the design of Intel's first microprocessors, and Ralph Ungermann, another Intel manager. With money from the oil giant Exxon's venture arm, they set out to build a better microprocessor than Intel's 8080.`,how:`Zilog designed processors and support chips and licensed other firms to make copies, so customers had more than one source. Its plan was to run the 8080's programs while adding more instructions and making computers simpler and cheaper to build.`,history:`Faggin and Ungermann founded Zilog in 1974, joined by Masatoshi Shima, who had co-designed the Intel 4004 and 8080. The Z80 of July 1976 ran all of the 8080's programs and needed only a single 5-volt power supply. It became the heart of the TRS-80 of 1977 and of many business computers running CP/M. Its 16-bit Z8000 of 1979 lost out to Intel's 8086 and Motorola's 68000. The power-chip maker IXYS bought Zilog in 2010. In 2024 Zilog announced the end of production of the original Z80.`,facts:[`The Z80 had about 8,500 transistors (1976).`,`A Z80 ran the Sinclair ZX Spectrum, a bestseller in Britain (1982).`,`The original Z80 was made for 48 years, until 2024.`]},"company:univacuum":{intro:`Modelled on UNIVAC, the computer business started in Philadelphia by J Presper Eckert and John Mauchly, the engineers who had built the ENIAC computer for the US Army during the war. Their company set out to sell the UNIVAC, a universal automatic computer for business and government that stored data on magnetic tape instead of punched cards.`,how:`Eckert and Mauchly built electronic computers to order, taking deposits from customers such as the Census Bureau to pay for the work. Each machine filled a room with thousands of vacuum tubes, and the company also had to invent its own tape drives, printers and ways of checking for errors.`,history:`Eckert and Mauchly left the University of Pennsylvania in 1946 and formed their company in 1947. Short of money, they sold it to the typewriter maker Remington Rand in 1950, and the first UNIVAC I was accepted by the US Census Bureau in March 1951. Remington Rand also bought Engineering Research Associates of St Paul, Minnesota, in 1952. On election night in 1952 a UNIVAC on CBS television predicted Dwight Eisenhower's landslide from early returns, and its name became a byword for computer. Remington Rand merged with Sperry in 1955 to form Sperry Rand. In 1986 Sperry merged with Burroughs to form Unisys.`,facts:[`A UNIVAC I used about 5,000 vacuum tubes (1951).`,`Forty-six UNIVAC I machines were built (1951 to 1956).`]},"company:controlfreak":{intro:`Modelled on Control Data Corporation, founded in Minneapolis by William Norris and other engineers who left Sperry Rand's UNIVAC division, among them the brilliant designer Seymour Cray. It set out to build the fastest scientific computers for laboratories, weather services and the military.`,how:`Control Data sold powerful machines to customers who cared most about raw speed, such as weapons laboratories and universities, competing with IBM by out-engineering it rather than out-selling it. Cray's designs used transistors instead of vacuum tubes and aimed for simplicity and speed.`,history:`Norris founded Control Data in 1957, raising money by selling shares directly to the public. Its CDC 1604 of 1960, designed by Cray, was one of the first fully transistorised computers. The CDC 6600 of 1964, built by Cray's team in Chippewa Falls, Wisconsin, was the fastest computer in the world, and IBM's chief Thomas Watson Jr asked his staff how a team of 34 people, including the janitor, had beaten IBM. In 1968 Control Data sued IBM for unfair competition. Cray left to found his own company in 1972, and in 1973 the settlement with IBM handed Control Data IBM's Service Bureau Corporation. After heavy losses Control Data was broken up in 1992.`,facts:[`The CDC 6600 did about 3 million calculations a second (1964).`,`About 100 CDC 6600s were sold (1964 to 1969).`]},"company:crayon":{intro:`Modelled on Cray Research, the company Seymour Cray founded in his home town of Chippewa Falls, Wisconsin, after leaving Control Data, to build nothing but the fastest computers in the world. Cray was intensely private and designed with a tiny team; by legend he dug a tunnel under his house to help him think.`,how:`Cray Research sold a handful of supercomputers a year, each costing millions of dollars, to weapons laboratories, weather forecasters, oil companies and car makers who needed to simulate physics. Its machines won through short wires, clever cooling and vector processing, which applies one instruction to a long list of numbers at once.`,history:`Seymour Cray founded the company in 1972. In 1976 the first Cray-1 went to the Los Alamos weapons laboratory: a C-shaped tower with a cushioned bench around its base that hid the power supplies and Freon cooling, able to do about 160 million calculations a second. The Cray X-MP of 1982, designed by Steve Chen, linked several processors together. The Cray-2 of 1985 was cooled by bathing its circuits in liquid. Seymour Cray left in 1989 to found Cray Computer Corporation. Silicon Graphics bought Cray Research in 1996.`,facts:[`A Cray-1 cost about $8 million (1976).`,`More than 80 Cray-1s were sold (1976 to 1982).`]},"company:decaf":{intro:`Modelled on Digital Equipment Corporation, founded by Ken Olsen and Harlan Anderson, engineers from MIT's Lincoln Laboratory, in part of an old woollen mill in Maynard, Massachusetts. Its first products were circuit modules for laboratories, and it aimed to build smaller, cheaper computers that people could use directly rather than through teams of operators.`,how:`DEC sold its machines outright at low prices to scientists, engineers and other manufacturers, who often built them into their own products. Rather than doing everything for customers as IBM did, it published its designs and let users write their own software, and it called its machines programmed data processors to avoid scaring investors with the word computer.`,history:`Olsen and Anderson founded DEC in 1957 with $70,000 from the venture firm American Research and Development. Its PDP-1 of 1959 sold for about $120,000, and in 1962 students at MIT wrote Spacewar!, an early video game, on one. The PDP-8 of 1965, priced at $18,000, was the first successful minicomputer. The PDP-11 of 1970 sold in huge numbers, and the Unix operating system grew up on it. Its VAX of 1977 helped make DEC the second-largest computer maker after IBM by the 1980s. In 1995 it launched the AltaVista search engine, but it had missed the PC, and in 1998 Compaq bought it for $9.6 billion.`,facts:[`DEC employed about 120,000 people (1987).`,`Compaq paid $9.6 billion for DEC (1998).`]},"company:datageneric":{intro:`Modelled on Data General, founded in Massachusetts by Edson de Castro and two other engineers from DEC, with Herbert Richman from Fairchild, after DEC turned down de Castro's design for a 16-bit minicomputer. It set out to beat DEC on price with a simpler, cheaper machine.`,how:`Data General built its computers on large printed circuit boards, which cut the cost of wiring, and sold them cheaply to manufacturers who resold them inside their own systems. It advertised brashly and drove its engineers very hard.`,history:`De Castro, Henry Burkhardt and Richard Sogge founded Data General in 1968. Its Nova of 1969 sold for $3,995. Its Eclipse range followed in 1974. In 1980 a team raced to build the 32-bit Eclipse MV/8000, a story told in Tracy Kidder's book The Soul of a New Machine, which won the Pulitzer Prize in 1982. In 1989 it launched the AViiON Unix servers, and its CLARiiON disk storage became its best business in the 1990s. The storage company EMC bought Data General for about $1.1 billion in 1999.`,facts:[`The Nova cost $3,995 (1969).`,`EMC paid about $1.1 billion for Data General (1999).`]},"company:packrat":{intro:`Modelled on Hewlett-Packard, founded by two Stanford friends, Bill Hewlett and David Packard, in a rented garage in Palo Alto, California, with $538. Their first product was an audio oscillator, an instrument that makes precise tones for testing sound equipment, and they made their name with electronic test and measurement instruments.`,how:`HP sold precise instruments to engineers and laboratories at fair prices and ploughed its profits back into research. Its founders ran it in an unusually open style: managers walked around and talked to staff, workers shared in the profits, and the firm avoided borrowing.`,history:`Hewlett and Packard formed their partnership on 1 January 1939, and in 1940 Walt Disney bought eight of their oscillators for the film Fantasia. HP launched its first computer, the HP 2116A, in 1966 to control its instruments. The HP-35 of 1972 was the first scientific pocket calculator. Its LaserJet printer of 1984 was a huge hit with offices. It bought Compaq in 2002 for about $25 billion, becoming the biggest PC maker. In 2015 it split in two: HP Inc for PCs and printers, and Hewlett Packard Enterprise for servers and services.`,facts:[`The HP-35 calculator cost $395 (1972).`,`California named the HP garage the birthplace of Silicon Valley (1989).`]},"company:sunburn":{intro:`Modelled on Sun Microsystems, founded by three Stanford graduates, Andy Bechtolsheim, Vinod Khosla and Scott McNealy, joined by the Berkeley Unix programmer Bill Joy. Its name came from the Stanford University Network, for which Bechtolsheim had designed a workstation built from standard parts.`,how:`Sun built powerful desktop workstations for engineers and scientists, running Unix and wired to networks from the start, and sold them for far less than minicomputers. It used off-the-shelf processors and open standards rather than designing everything itself.`,history:`Sun was founded in February 1982 and shipped the Sun-1 that year. In 1984 it released NFS, which let computers share files over a network. In 1987 it brought out its own SPARC processor design. Its Java programming language, launched in May 1995, promised software that could be written once and run anywhere. During the dot-com boom of 2000 its servers ran much of the web and it was worth about $200 billion, but cheap Linux PCs undercut it after the crash. Oracle bought Sun for $7.4 billion in January 2010.`,facts:[`Sun's stock market value peaked at about $200 billion (2000).`,`Oracle paid $7.4 billion for Sun (2010).`]},"company:sgraffiti":{intro:`Modelled on Silicon Graphics, founded by Jim Clark, a Stanford professor, and several of his students around the Geometry Engine, a chip that did the heavy maths of 3D graphics in hardware. It set out to build workstations that could draw and rotate 3D images in real time.`,how:`Silicon Graphics sold expensive workstations and servers with graphics hardware far ahead of anything else, to film studios, car and aircraft designers, scientists and the military. Its machines ran its own version of Unix and its IRIS GL software library for drawing 3D scenes.`,history:`Clark founded Silicon Graphics in November 1981. Its first IRIS graphics terminals shipped in 1983. In 1992 it turned its graphics library into the open OpenGL standard and bought the processor designer MIPS. Its computers created the dinosaurs of Jurassic Park in 1993, and that year it agreed to design the graphics chip of the Nintendo 64. Clark left in 1994 to found Netscape, and in 1996 SGI bought Cray Research. Cheap PC graphics cards undercut its expensive machines in the late 1990s.`,facts:[`The Nintendo 64's graphics came from SGI's Reality Coprocessor (1996).`,`Google moved into SGI's old headquarters, which became the Googleplex (2003).`,`After bankruptcy, what remained of SGI was sold for about $25 million (2009).`]},"company:mipsy":{intro:`Modelled on MIPS Computer Systems, founded by Stanford professor John Hennessy and colleagues to sell a RISC processor based on his university research. RISC, short for reduced instruction set computing, uses a small set of simple instructions that run very fast, instead of many complicated ones.`,how:`MIPS designed processors and licensed chipmakers such as NEC, Toshiba and Siemens to make and sell them, while also building its own workstations and servers around them. Its compilers did the clever work the hardware left out, so the chip could stay simple and fast.`,history:`Hennessy co-founded MIPS in 1984. Its R2000 processor appeared in 1986 and the faster R3000 in 1988. Silicon Graphics, its biggest customer, bought it in 1992, a year after the 64-bit R4000 appeared. Its designs went into games consoles: the Sony PlayStation of 1994 used an R3000-based chip and the Nintendo 64 of 1996 an R4300i. SGI spun MIPS Technologies off in 1998 as a firm that only licensed designs. After passing through several owners, it switched to the RISC-V instruction set in 2021.`,facts:[`The PlayStation 2's Emotion Engine was built around a MIPS core (2000).`,`Hennessy shared the Turing Award with David Patterson for their work on RISC (2017).`,`The chipmaker GlobalFoundries agreed to buy MIPS (2025).`]},"company:commadore":{intro:`Modelled on Commodore, founded in Toronto by Jack Tramiel, a Polish-born survivor of Auschwitz, as a typewriter repair and import business. Commodore Portable Typewriter assembled and sold cheap typewriters, and Tramiel's motto was that business is war.`,how:`Commodore sold office machines at low prices, cutting costs ruthlessly and squeezing its suppliers. Tramiel moved quickly from one product to the next, chasing whatever could be sold cheaply in large numbers.`,history:`Tramiel founded Commodore in Toronto in 1954. In the 1960s it moved into adding machines and then calculators. In 1976 it bought the chipmaker MOS Technology, and its PET of 1977 was one of the first complete personal computers. The VIC-20 of 1981 was cheap enough for families, and the Commodore 64 of 1982 was launched at $595. Tramiel left in 1984, the year Commodore bought the start-up Amiga, and the Amiga 1000 of 1985 had graphics and sound far ahead of its rivals. Poor management and the rise of the PC brought bankruptcy in April 1994.`,facts:[`The VIC-20 was the first computer to sell 1 million units (1982).`,`The Commodore 64 sold an estimated 12.5 to 17 million units (1982 to 1994).`]},"company:radioshock":{intro:`Modelled on Tandy and its Radio Shack chain, after the Fort Worth leather-goods businessman Charles Tandy bought Radio Shack, a struggling Boston retailer of radio and electronics parts. Tandy set out to turn it into a nationwide chain of small, cheap shops selling own-brand radios, hi-fi parts, batteries and kits to hobbyists.`,how:`Radio Shack sold mostly its own brands, made cheaply for it in Asia or in Tandy's own factories, so it kept the profit a middleman would take. Small shops in every town, a free battery club and constant catalogues brought customers back.`,history:`Radio Shack opened in Boston in 1921, and Tandy bought it in 1963. In August 1977 it launched the TRS-80, one of the first ready-built personal computers, priced at $599.95 with a monitor and tape recorder, and sold it through its thousands of stores. The TRS-80 Model 100 of 1983 was among the first laptop computers, and the IBM-compatible Tandy 1000 of 1984 was a bestseller. In 1993 Tandy sold its computer factories to AST Research. In 2000 the company renamed itself RadioShack. It went bankrupt in 2015 and again in 2017.`,facts:[`Radio Shack had nine stores when Tandy bought it (1963).`,`The TRS-80 Model 100 ran for about 20 hours on four AA batteries (1983).`]},"company:acorny":{intro:`Modelled on Acorn Computers, founded in Cambridge, England, by Hermann Hauser, an Austrian physicist, and Chris Curry, who had worked for the inventor Clive Sinclair. It began as CPU Ltd, a consultancy designing microprocessor controllers, and sold small computer kits to electronics hobbyists.`,how:`Acorn designed its own computers and paid others to build them, hiring bright young Cambridge graduates such as Sophie Wilson and Steve Furber. It aimed at schools and families, with machines that were well built and easy to program in its own version of BASIC.`,history:`Hauser and Curry founded the business in 1978. In 1981 it won the contract for the BBC Micro, the machine of the BBC's Computer Literacy Project, which went into most British schools. Heavy losses on the cheaper Electron led the Italian firm Olivetti to take control in 1985, the year Wilson and Furber's first ARM processor came back from the factory and worked first time. The Archimedes of 1987 was built around ARM. In 1990 Acorn spun the chip design off into ARM Ltd with Apple and VLSI Technology. Acorn itself was broken up between 1998 and 2000.`,facts:[`The Acorn Atom home computer went on sale (1980).`,`About 1.5 million BBC Micros were sold (1981 to 1994).`,`The first ARM chip had about 25,000 transistors (1985).`]},"company:clonepaq":{intro:`Modelled on Compaq Computer, founded in Houston by three Texas Instruments managers, Rod Canion, Jim Harris and Bill Murto, who sketched their first product on a placemat in a pie restaurant. Their plan was a portable computer that ran all the software written for the IBM PC.`,how:`Compaq could not copy IBM's BIOS, the built-in code that starts the PC, so engineers who had never seen IBM's version wrote a compatible one from its published specifications, a clean-room method that kept it safe from lawsuits. It sold through dealers at prices close to IBM's, competing on quality and speed.`,history:`Canion, Harris and Murto founded Compaq in February 1982. The Compaq Portable, a suitcase-sized PC, shipped in 1983 and made $111 million in its first year, a record for a new American company. In September 1986 its Deskpro 386 beat IBM to market with Intel's new 386 processor. Compaq was the world's biggest PC maker by 1994. In 1998 it bought Digital Equipment Corporation for $9.6 billion. Hewlett-Packard bought Compaq in 2002 for about $25 billion.`,facts:[`Compaq's first-year sales were $111 million (1983).`,`The Compaq Portable weighed about 13 kilograms (1983).`]},"company:dull":{intro:`Modelled on Dell, which Michael Dell started as PC's Limited in his room at the University of Texas in Austin, upgrading and selling IBM-compatible computers. He sold directly to customers through mail-order adverts and the telephone, cutting out the shops.`,how:`Dell built each computer to the customer's order and shipped it straight to them, so it held little stock, never got stuck with outdated parts and was paid before it paid its suppliers. Selling direct also gave it a close view of what customers wanted.`,history:`Michael Dell founded PC's Limited in 1984 with $1,000. He renamed it Dell Computer in 1987 and floated it in 1988. In 1996 it began selling through its website, and by 1997 it was taking about $1 million a day online. It became the world's largest PC maker in 2001. Michael Dell took the company private in 2013 for about $25 billion. In 2016 it bought the storage giant EMC, with its stake in VMware.`,facts:[`Michael Dell was 19 when he started the company (1984).`,`Dell paid about $67 billion for EMC, then the largest technology deal ever (2016).`]},"company:lenono":{intro:`Modelled on Lenovo, which began as Legend, a company founded in a small guardhouse in Beijing by Liu Chuanzhi and ten other engineers from the Chinese Academy of Sciences, with 200,000 yuan of state money. It started by reselling foreign computers and televisions and offering repairs.`,how:`Legend made its first money by distributing imported computers, and its engineers developed a card that let IBM-compatible PCs handle Chinese characters. It planned to use its trading profits to build PCs of its own for the Chinese market.`,history:`Liu and his colleagues founded the company in 1984. Its Legend Chinese character card of 1985 became a hit. It listed in Hong Kong in 1994 and became China's biggest PC seller in 1997. It renamed itself Lenovo in 2003, and in 2005 it bought IBM's PC business, including the ThinkPad laptops, for about $1.75 billion. In 2013 it became the world's largest PC maker, and in 2014 it bought Motorola Mobility from Google and IBM's x86 server business.`,facts:[`Lenovo paid about $2.9 billion for Motorola Mobility (2014).`,`It sold about 60 million PCs (2024).`]},"company:eclectic":{intro:`Modelled on NEC, Nippon Electric Company, founded in Tokyo as Japan's first joint venture with a foreign company, the American firm Western Electric, which held a majority stake. Led by Kunihiko Iwadare, it made and imported telephones and switchboards for Japan's growing telephone network.`,how:`NEC built communications equipment for Japan's state telephone service and the military, learning manufacturing methods from Western Electric. It grew by supplying exchanges, radio links and transmission equipment to the telephone monopoly, and those steady orders paid for its research.`,history:`NEC was founded on 17 July 1899. In 1959 it built the NEAC-2201, one of Japan's first transistor computers. Its PC-9801 of 1982 became Japan's standard PC, with its own Japanese-language software. By 1985 NEC was the world's biggest chipmaker. It merged its memory chips with Hitachi's into Elpida in 1999. In 2010 it merged its remaining chip business into Renesas.`,facts:[`Its chairman Koji Kobayashi set out the idea of C&C, computers and communications combined (1977).`,`More than 18 million PC-98 computers were sold (1982 to 2003).`,`Its Earth Simulator was the world's fastest supercomputer, at about 36 trillion calculations a second (2002).`]},"company:toastiba":{intro:`Modelled on Toshiba, the Tokyo company formed by merging Shibaura Engineering Works, which grew from the inventor Hisashige Tanaka's workshop, with Tokyo Electric, a maker of light bulbs. Called Tokyo Shibaura Electric, it made everything from heavy generators and electric motors to radios, bulbs and household appliances.`,how:`Toshiba was a sprawling industrial group in which power stations, trains and factory equipment sat beside consumer electronics. Profits from big contracts with the state and the power companies paid for research, and its laboratories produced many of Japan's first electrical products.`,history:`Tanaka's workshop opened in 1875, and the merger that created Tokyo Shibaura Electric took place in 1939. In 1984 it officially took the name Toshiba, and that year its engineer Fujio Masuoka presented flash memory, which keeps data without power. Its T1100 of 1985 was one of the first IBM-compatible laptop computers, and in 1986 it was among the first to mass-produce 1-megabit DRAM chips. NAND flash, cheaper to make in large sizes, followed in 1987. In 2015 an accounting scandal revealed years of inflated profits, and in 2017 losses at its American nuclear arm Westinghouse forced it to sell its memory business, which became Kioxia. It was taken private by a group of Japanese investors in 2023.`,facts:[`Its accounting scandal involved about $1.2 billion of overstated profits (2015).`,`Toshiba sold its memory business for about $18 billion (2018).`]},"company:hitachoo":{intro:`Modelled on Hitachi, founded by the engineer Namihei Odaira at a copper mine near the town of Hitachi, north of Tokyo, where he built five-horsepower electric motors to replace imported ones. It grew into a maker of generators, locomotives and heavy electrical machinery.`,how:`Hitachi built heavy equipment for power companies, railways and factories, insisting on developing its own technology rather than buying foreign designs. Its size let it enter new fields, from household goods to electronics, supported by the profits of the old ones.`,history:`Odaira built his first motors in 1910, and Hitachi became an independent company in 1920. It built its first computers in the 1950s. In 1982 several of its employees were arrested in an FBI sting for buying stolen IBM secrets. Its SuperH processors ran Sega's Saturn console of 1994. It bought IBM's hard-disk business in 2003. It made a record loss of 787 billion yen in the year to March 2009, then the biggest ever for a Japanese manufacturer, and refocused on infrastructure and IT services.`,facts:[`Hitachi paid about $2 billion for IBM's hard-disk business (2003).`,`Western Digital paid $4.8 billion for that disk business (2012).`]},"company:fujitsoon":{intro:`Modelled on Fujitsu, founded in Kawasaki as Fuji Tsushinki, the communications equipment arm of Fuji Electric, itself a partnership of the Japanese Furukawa group and Germany's Siemens. It made telephone exchanges and other equipment for Japan's state telephone service.`,how:`Fujitsu built telephone, telegraph and radio equipment, selling mostly to the state telephone service and other government bodies, which gave it steady orders. Its engineers learned from Siemens' designs while building up their own.`,history:`Fuji Tsushinki was founded in 1935. In 1954 it built the FACOM 100, one of Japan's first computers, using relays. By 1979 Fujitsu, which built mainframes that ran IBM's software with help from Gene Amdahl's American company, had passed IBM as the biggest computer seller in Japan. In 1990 it bought a controlling stake in the British computer maker ICL. From 1999 Britain's Post Office used ICL's Horizon accounting system, whose faulty data led to hundreds of sub-postmasters being wrongly prosecuted. Its K computer was the world's fastest in 2011.`,facts:[`The K computer passed 10 quadrillion calculations a second (2011).`,`Fugaku, built with the RIKEN institute, became the world's fastest supercomputer (2020).`,`Fugaku had more than 7.6 million processor cores (2020).`,`A television drama about the Horizon scandal caused national outrage in Britain (2024).`]},"company:elpidead":{intro:`Modelled on Elpida Memory, formed by merging the DRAM memory businesses of two Japanese giants, NEC and Hitachi, which were losing money to Korean rivals. Its name comes from the Greek word for hope, and it was meant to give Japan one strong maker of memory chips.`,how:`Elpida designed and made DRAM chips for PCs, servers and mobile phones, competing on cost with Samsung and Hynix in a market where prices could halve within a year. It needed huge and constant investment in new factories to keep up.`,history:`NEC and Hitachi set up the company in December 1999. It took the Elpida name in 2000. Yukio Sakamoto became chief executive in 2002, and in 2003 Elpida took over Mitsubishi Electric's DRAM business. After the financial crisis it received a government-backed rescue using public money in 2009. Crushed by falling prices and a strong yen, it filed for bankruptcy on 27 February 2012 with debts of about 448 billion yen, then the largest for a Japanese manufacturer since the Second World War. Micron completed its purchase of Elpida in July 2013.`,facts:[`Its rescue included 30 billion yen of public money (2009).`,`Elpida's debts were about 448 billion yen (2012).`]},"company:qimonday":{intro:`Modelled on Qimonda, the memory chip company that the German chipmaker Infineon spun off from its DRAM business, with headquarters in Munich and factories in Dresden and Richmond, Virginia. It was Europe's only big maker of DRAM memory chips.`,how:`Qimonda made DRAM chips for PCs, servers, graphics cards and games consoles, and was developing a new buried wordline cell design to shrink its chips. Like every memory maker it lived on prices that swung between boom and bust.`,history:`Infineon set up Qimonda in May 2006 and floated it on the New York Stock Exchange in August 2006. In 2007 and 2008 a glut of chips sent DRAM prices crashing, and Qimonda lost billions of euros. The German state of Saxony and Infineon offered a rescue package in December 2008, but it was not enough, and Qimonda filed for insolvency on 23 January 2009.`,facts:[`Its shares were floated at $13 each (2006).`,`The rescue offered in December 2008 totalled about €325 million.`]},"company:umc":{intro:`Modelled on United Microelectronics Corporation, Taiwan's first chip company, spun out of the government's Industrial Technology Research Institute in the new Hsinchu Science Park. It began by making chips of its own design for consumer electronics such as telephones and toys.`,how:`UMC designed and made its own chips while also manufacturing chips for other companies. The government gave it technology that the institute had licensed from the American firm RCA, aiming to start a home-grown chip industry.`,history:`UMC was founded in 1980. In 1985 it became the first chip company listed on Taiwan's stock exchange. In 1995 it turned itself into a pure foundry, making only other companies' designs, and set up joint ventures with American chip designers. In 2000 it merged five sister companies into itself and listed shares in New York. In 2018 it stopped racing for the most advanced chips, settling on older, cheaper processes. In 2020 it pleaded guilty in the US to stealing memory chip secrets from Micron and paid a $60 million fine.`,facts:[`UMC stopped developing processes finer than 12 nanometres (2018).`,`Its revenue was about NT$232 billion (2024).`]},"company:materialism":{intro:`Modelled on Applied Materials, founded in Mountain View, California, by Michael McNeilly and a few partners to make equipment for chip factories. It started with machines for chemical vapour deposition, which grows thin films of material on silicon wafers from reacting gases.`,how:`Applied Materials sold the machines that lay down and etch away the layers of a chip, along with spare parts and service contracts that kept paying long after each sale. Every new generation of chips needed new tools, so its fortunes rose and fell with chipmakers' spending.`,history:`Applied Materials was founded in November 1967 to make deposition equipment for chipmakers. Jim Morgan, who took charge in 1976, steered it through a slump, and in 1979 it set up a joint venture in Japan, one of the first American chip-tool firms to sell there. Its Precision 5000 of 1987 put several processing chambers around one robot that moved wafers between them without exposing them to air. It became the largest maker of chip-making equipment in 1992. A planned merger with its Japanese rival Tokyo Electron was called off in 2015 over competition concerns.`,facts:[`It first sold shares to the public (1972).`,`Jim Morgan led the company from 1976 to 2003.`,`Its revenue was about $27 billion in its 2024 financial year.`]},"company:nikoff":{intro:`Modelled on Nikon, founded in Tokyo as Nippon Kogaku, the Japan Optical Company, when three optical firms merged with the backing of the Mitsubishi group. It made rangefinders, periscopes, binoculars and lenses, much of it for the Imperial Japanese Navy.`,how:`Nippon Kogaku made precision optics, grinding and testing glass to tight tolerances and designing its own lenses. Those skills let it turn from military work to cameras, microscopes and surveying instruments.`,history:`Nippon Kogaku was founded on 25 July 1917. Its first camera, the Nikon I, appeared in 1948. In 1950 American photographers covering the Korean War discovered its lenses, making its name abroad. The Nikon F of 1959 quickly became the favourite of press photographers. In 1980 it launched the NSR-1010G, Japan's first commercial wafer stepper for printing chip patterns, and its steppers went on to lead the world market in the 1980s. In the 2000s it lost the lithography market to ASML.`,facts:[`The Nikon F stayed in production until 1973, 14 years after its launch.`,`ASML and Zeiss agreed to pay Nikon €150 million to settle patent disputes (2019).`]},"company:cannon":{intro:`Modelled on Canon, founded in Tokyo as Precision Optical Industry by Goro Yoshida, Saburo Uchida and Takeshi Mitarai to build a Japanese rival to Germany's Leica cameras. Its first prototype was named Kwanon, after the Buddhist goddess of mercy, and the name became the brand Canon.`,how:`Canon designed precision cameras, at first buying its lenses from Nippon Kogaku, and set out to match German quality at lower prices. Its founders believed Japanese engineering could match the best in Europe.`,history:`Precision Optical Industry was formally founded in 1937, after selling its first Hansa Canon camera in 1936. It renamed itself Canon Camera in 1947. In 1970 it made Japan's first mask aligner for printing chip patterns, the PPC-1, along with its first plain-paper copier, the NP-1100. Its AE-1 of 1976 was the first camera with a microprocessor. Its laser engine powered HP's first LaserJet printer in 1984. In 2023 it launched a nanoimprint machine that presses chip patterns into the wafer like a stamp.`,facts:[`Canon's first prototype camera, the Kwanon, was made in 1934.`,`Its FPA steppers were strong in i-line lithography (1990s).`,`Its nanoimprint machine could print lines just 14 nanometres wide (2023).`]},"company:gcamann":{intro:`Modelled on GCA, the Geophysics Corporation of America, a Massachusetts research firm, and its David W Mann division, a maker of precision optical instruments. Mann's photorepeaters, cameras that print the same tiny chip pattern again and again onto a glass plate, made it a key supplier to the young chip industry.`,how:`Mann's machines made the photomasks that chipmakers used to print their circuits. A photorepeater shrank a drawing of one chip and stepped it across a glass plate in a grid, so that a whole wafer of identical chips could then be printed at once.`,history:`The Geophysics Corporation of America was founded in Bedford, Massachusetts, in 1958. Mann's photorepeater of 1961 became a standard tool for making chip masks. In 1978 GCA began selling the DSW 4800, the first commercial wafer stepper, which printed the pattern straight onto the wafer one chip at a time instead of through a full-size mask. Its steppers led the market in the early 1980s, but Nikon and Canon overtook it with more reliable machines by the middle of the decade. After heavy losses it was sold to General Signal in 1988. General Signal closed it in 1993.`,facts:[`The DSW in its stepper's name stood for direct step on wafer (1978).`,`GCA closed despite help from the US chip consortium SEMATECH (1993).`]},"company:perkinelmo":{intro:`Modelled on Perkin-Elmer, founded in New York by Richard Perkin, an investment banker, and Charles Elmer, a retired court reporter and keen amateur astronomer, to make precision optics. In the war its lenses and optics went into bombsights, rangefinders and reconnaissance cameras.`,how:`Perkin-Elmer designed and built precise optical systems and scientific instruments, such as spectrophotometers that identify chemicals by the light they absorb. Government and military contracts paid for much of its work and kept its optical engineers at the leading edge.`,history:`Perkin and Elmer founded the company in 1937. In 1973 it launched the Micralign, a projection aligner built around a mirror design by Abe Offner, which printed chip patterns without the mask touching the wafer, sharply raising chip yields. It finished grinding the main mirror of NASA's Hubble Space Telescope in 1981. It sold its lithography arm to SVG in 1990. After Hubble's launch in 1990 the mirror proved to have been shaped wrongly, blurring its pictures until astronauts fitted corrective optics in 1993. In 2001 ASML bought SVG, and with it the line of machines that began with the Micralign.`,facts:[`More than 2,000 Micralign machines were sold after its launch (1973).`,`Hubble's mirror was too flat at its edge by about 2 micrometres (1990).`]},"company:seagull":{intro:`Modelled on Seagate Technology, founded in Scotts Valley, California, as Shugart Technology by Alan Shugart, a disk-drive pioneer from IBM, with Finis Conner, Tom Mitchell, Doug Mahon and Syed Iftikar. It set out to build a small hard disk drive that would fit into the space of a 5.25-inch floppy drive, for the new microcomputers.`,how:`Seagate made hard disks, spinning metal platters read by heads that float a hair's breadth above them, and sold them to computer makers. It kept costs down so it could sell in huge volumes at falling prices.`,history:`Shugart and his partners founded the company in 1979. It was renamed Seagate in 1980, when it launched the ST-506, a 5-megabyte drive for $1,500. In 1982 it opened a factory in Singapore, and in 1983 IBM chose its 10-megabyte ST-412 for the IBM PC XT. It merged with Conner Peripherals in 1996. In 2006 it bought its rival Maxtor. In 2024 it began shipping drives of more than 30 terabytes using heat-assisted magnetic recording, which warms each spot with a laser before writing to it.`,facts:[`The ST-506 held 5 megabytes and cost $1,500 (1980).`,`A 30-terabyte drive held 6 million times as much as the ST-506 (2024).`]},"company:westerndigit":{intro:`Modelled on Western Digital, founded in Southern California as General Digital by Alvin Phillips, a former Motorola employee, to make MOS chips and test equipment. It became a maker of chips for electronic calculators, one of the hottest markets of the time.`,how:`Western Digital designed and made specialised chips for other manufacturers, at first for calculators. Its plan was to find new niches as each market matured, rather than compete head-on with the biggest chipmakers.`,history:`Phillips founded General Digital in 1970, and it soon took the name Western Digital. When the calculator market collapsed and a big customer went bust, it filed for bankruptcy protection in 1976, then recovered with its WD1771 floppy disk controller chip that year. Its disk controller cards were used in many IBM PCs in the 1980s. In 1988 it bought Tandon's disk drive business and began making hard drives itself. It bought Hitachi's disk business in 2012. In 2016 it bought the flash memory maker SanDisk.`,facts:[`It paid $4.8 billion for Hitachi's disk business (2012).`,`It paid about $19 billion for SanDisk (2016).`]},"company:sanddisk":{intro:`Modelled on SanDisk, founded as SunDisk by Eli Harari, an expert in memory chips that keep data without power, with Sanjay Mehrotra and Jack Yuan. It set out to replace spinning hard disks with flash memory, which has no moving parts.`,how:`SanDisk designed flash memory systems, pairing memory chips with controller chips that spread wear evenly and hid faults, and licensed its many patents to other memory makers. It sold memory cards and drives to camera, phone and computer makers and in shops.`,history:`Harari, Mehrotra and Yuan founded SunDisk in 1988. In 1991 it shipped a 20-megabyte flash drive for IBM, the first flash-based solid-state disk. It introduced the CompactFlash card in 1994, renamed itself SanDisk in 1995 and floated that November. In 1999 it created the SD card with Panasonic and Toshiba, and in the 2000s it shared NAND flash factories in Japan with Toshiba. Western Digital bought it for about $19 billion in 2016. It was spun off again as a separate company in February 2025.`,facts:[`Its first solid-state disk held 20 megabytes (1991).`,`Western Digital paid about $19 billion for SanDisk (2016).`]},"company:armadillo":{intro:`Modelled on ARM, Advanced RISC Machines, set up in Cambridge, England, as a joint venture of Acorn Computers, Apple and the chipmaker VLSI Technology, with 12 engineers from Acorn working in a converted barn. It took over the ARM processor design and set out to make it a standard for chips that need little power.`,how:`ARM makes no chips. It designs processor cores and licenses them to chip companies for an upfront fee plus a royalty on every chip they sell, so its designs spread across many firms and products. Its processors were small and frugal with power, which suited battery-powered gadgets.`,history:`ARM was founded in November 1990 under chief executive Robin Saxby. Its ARM6 powered Apple's Newton handheld in 1993. Its ARM7TDMI went into Nokia phones such as the 6110 of 1997. It floated in London and New York in 1998. The Japanese group SoftBank bought it for £24 billion in 2016. After NVIDIA's $40 billion bid for it collapsed in 2022, it floated again in New York in September 2023.`,facts:[`Apple put about $3 million into ARM at its founding (1990).`,`The ARM7TDMI also powered Nintendo's Game Boy Advance (2001).`,`More than 250 billion chips based on ARM designs had been shipped (2023).`]},"company:atitude":{intro:`Modelled on ATI Technologies, founded near Toronto as Array Technologies Incorporated by Kwok Yuen Ho, Lee Lau and Benny Lau, immigrants from Hong Kong. It made graphics chips and add-in boards that let IBM-compatible PCs show sharper, more colourful pictures, selling them to PC makers and in shops.`,how:`ATI designed graphics chips and sold them on its own add-in cards and to PC makers, competing on features and price. Its chips were made by outside foundries, and it did well selling to big computer makers who bought in volume.`,history:`Ho and the Laus founded the company in 1985 to make graphics boards for PCs. Its Mach8 of 1991 sped up Windows. Its Rage chips of 1996 added 3D graphics. In 2000 it bought ArtX, which had designed the graphics of Nintendo's GameCube, and its Radeon 9700 of 2002 beat NVIDIA's best cards. It made the graphics chips of Microsoft's Xbox 360 in 2005 and Nintendo's Wii in 2006. AMD bought ATI for $5.4 billion in 2006.`,facts:[`Its Small Wonder graphics card appeared (1987).`,`The Radeon 9700 was the first card to support DirectX 9 (2002).`,`AMD retired the ATI name (2010).`]},"company:threedflux":{intro:`Modelled on 3dfx Interactive, founded in San Jose, California, by Ross Smith, Gary Tarolli and Scott Sellers, engineers who had worked at Silicon Graphics. They set out to bring arcade-quality 3D graphics to ordinary PCs, just as the price of fast memory chips was falling.`,how:`3dfx designed graphics chips that did only 3D, drawing textured triangles very fast, and sold them first to arcade-game makers and then to makers of PC add-in cards. It relied on cheap memory chips and a lean design rather than trying to do everything on one chip.`,history:`Smith, Tarolli and Sellers founded 3dfx in 1994. Its Voodoo Graphics chip reached PC cards in late 1996, sitting beside the normal graphics card and making 3D games look far better. By 1997 many PC games supported its own Glide programming interface. Its Voodoo2 of 1998 let two cards share the work, a method it called SLI. In December 1998 it bought the card maker STB Systems to build its own boards, which drove away the companies that had made cards for it. NVIDIA's faster GeForce chips overtook it, and in December 2000 NVIDIA bought its patents and staff; 3dfx filed for bankruptcy in 2002.`,facts:[`Two Voodoo2 cards together could show 1024 by 768 pixels (1998).`,`NVIDIA paid about $70 million in cash for its assets (2000).`]},"company:palmy":{intro:`Modelled on Palm Computing, founded by Jeff Hawkins, an engineer fascinated by how the brain works, with Donna Dubinsky as chief executive. It began by writing software for handheld computers built by others, such as the Zoomer made with Casio and Tandy.`,how:`Palm wrote handwriting recognition and personal organiser software, and Hawkins believed a handheld had to do a few things well and fit in a shirt pocket. Its plan was to earn money from software sold with devices built by partners.`,history:`Hawkins founded Palm in 1992, and its first partner device, the Zoomer, was being built with Casio and Tandy. In 1994 it released Graffiti, a simplified alphabet that computers could recognise easily, and in 1995 the modem maker US Robotics bought it. The PalmPilot of March 1996, priced at $299, was one of the fastest-selling computer products ever, with over a million sold in about 18 months. In 1998 Hawkins and Dubinsky left to found Handspring, and in 2000 Palm floated. Its Treo smartphones of 2003 were popular. After the iPhone outclassed it, Hewlett-Packard bought Palm for $1.2 billion in 2010.`,facts:[`Palm's stock market value passed $50 billion on its first day of trading (2000).`,`HP paid $1.2 billion for Palm (2010).`]},"company:blueberry":{intro:`Modelled on Research In Motion, the company that Mike Lazaridis, an engineering student at the University of Waterloo, founded with his friend Douglas Fregin in Waterloo, Ontario. It began by making electronic display signs and doing engineering contracts for other firms.`,how:`RIM designed hardware and software for sending data over radio networks, doing contract engineering for other firms and for wireless network operators. It aimed to build devices that kept businesspeople connected while they were away from their desks.`,history:`Lazaridis and Fregin founded RIM in 1984. In 1999 it launched the BlackBerry, a pager with a tiny thumb keyboard that pushed office emails to the device as they arrived. Its first BlackBerry phones appeared in 2002. In 2006 it paid $612.5 million to settle a patent case that had threatened to shut the service down. Its stock market value peaked at about $83 billion in 2008, before the iPhone and Android phones overtook it. It renamed itself BlackBerry in 2013 and stopped designing phones in 2016 to concentrate on security software.`,facts:[`Jim Balsillie joined as co-chief executive (1992).`,`BlackBerry had about 80 million users at its peak (2012).`]},"company:cuc":{intro:`Modelled on the Computer Usage Company, founded in New York by two former IBM employees, Elmer Kubie and John Sheldon, with $40,000. It was the first company set up to sell programming, writing software for clients who had bought or rented computers but lacked people who knew how to program them.`,how:`CUC sold its programmers' time: it wrote custom programs for companies and government agencies, ran jobs on rented computer time and trained clients' staff. Software was not yet sold as a product, so every program was written to order.`,history:`Kubie and Sheldon founded the Computer Usage Company in March 1955, and its first project that year was a program for the California Research Corporation to simulate the flow of oil. It sold shares to the public in April 1960, among the first software firms to do so. In 1962 Cuthbert Hurd, who had led IBM's early computer sales, joined as chairman. It grew through the 1960s, but computer makers and new rivals were offering programming too. It was declared bankrupt in 1986.`,facts:[`CUC started with $40,000, enough for its founders and a staff of five (1955).`,`It lasted 31 years before going bankrupt (1986).`]},"company:sdc":{intro:`Modelled on the System Development Corporation of Santa Monica, California, spun out of the RAND Corporation, a US Air Force think tank, to write the software for SAGE, the computerised air defence network meant to spot Soviet bombers. It was a non-profit company and had to train most of its programmers itself.`,how:`SDC wrote programs under government contracts, mainly for the Air Force. Because almost no one could yet program, it gave aptitude tests to people from many backgrounds and taught them, and its work covered huge real-time systems linking radar stations, computers and human operators.`,history:`RAND's System Development Division became the separate System Development Corporation in 1956. SAGE's first direction centre went into service in 1958, running programs SDC had written for IBM's huge AN/FSQ-7 computers. In 1959 its programmer Jules Schwartz created the JOVIAL programming language. In 1963 SDC ran one of the first time-sharing systems, which let many users share one computer at once, on the Q-32. It became a profit-making company in 1969. The computer maker Burroughs bought it in 1980.`,facts:[`Each AN/FSQ-7 computer used about 55,000 vacuum tubes (1958).`,`SAGE's programs ran to hundreds of thousands of instructions (1958).`]},"company:patentresearch":{intro:`Modelled on Applied Data Research, founded in Princeton, New Jersey, by a group of programmers to write software for computer makers and computer users. It wrote programs under contract at a time when almost all software came free with the machine.`,how:`ADR earned its money from contract programming, writing systems software and applications for computer makers such as RCA and for large companies. Its programmers looked for tasks that many customers had in common and that could be solved once and sold many times.`,history:`ADR was founded in 1959. In 1965 it launched Autoflow, a program that drew flowcharts of existing programs, one of the first software products sold commercially. In 1968 its Martin Goetz received one of the first US patents for software, for a sorting program. In 1969 ADR sued IBM for giving software away free with its computers, and IBM began charging for software separately that year. Ameritech bought ADR in 1986 and sold it to Computer Associates in 1988.`,facts:[`IBM paid ADR about $2 million to settle its lawsuit (1970).`,`Goetz's patent was granted for a sorting system (1968).`]},"company:seances":{intro:`Modelled on Computer Sciences Corporation, founded in Los Angeles by Fletcher Jones, a computer salesman, and Roy Nutt, one of the IBM team that had written the first FORTRAN compiler, with $100 between them. It set out to write programming languages and system software for computer makers.`,how:`CSC wrote compilers and other software under contract for computer makers and government agencies, charging for its programmers' time. It took on large, fixed-price projects, betting that its experts could finish them faster than the customers' own staff.`,history:`Jones and Nutt founded CSC in April 1959, and its first big contract was a business compiler called FACT for Honeywell. It grew through the 1960s on work for NASA and the defence department, becoming one of the largest software firms in the United States. Fletcher Jones died in a plane crash in 1972. In the 1990s CSC became one of the biggest outsourcing firms, running whole computer systems for companies and governments. In April 2017 it merged with Hewlett Packard Enterprise's services arm to form DXC Technology.`,facts:[`Its founders started it with $100 (1959).`,`DXC Technology began with about 170,000 staff (2017).`]},"company:informatricks":{intro:`Modelled on Informatics Inc, founded in the Los Angeles area by Walter Bauer and three colleagues to write software for the military and for computer users. It was one of the first independent software houses in California.`,how:`Informatics earned its money from contract programming for the government and big companies, and it looked for programs it could sell many times. It sold software under licence, charging each customer a fee to use a copy.`,history:`Bauer, Werner Frank, Richard Hill and Frank Wagner founded Informatics in 1962. In 1967 it began selling Mark IV, a file management and report program that let business staff produce reports without writing code, for IBM's System/360 computers. After IBM began charging for software separately in 1969, Mark IV became the first software package to pass $1 million in sales. In 1985 Sterling Software bought Informatics in a hostile takeover.`,facts:[`Mark IV was the first software product to pass $10 million in total sales (1970s).`,`It was also the first to pass $100 million in total sales (1980s).`]},"company:sincom":{intro:`Modelled on Cincom Systems, founded in Cincinnati, Ohio, by Tom Nies, a young former IBM salesman, with two partners. Its first product was TOTAL, a database management system: software that stores a company's records and links related ones so programs can find them quickly.`,how:`Cincom licensed TOTAL to companies running IBM and other mainframes, charging fees for the software and for support. TOTAL linked records in chains, which was fast but meant programmers had to know the paths through the data in advance.`,history:`Nies, Claude Bogardus and Tom Richley founded Cincom in 1968 and released TOTAL that year, one of the first database products sold to the public. Because TOTAL ran on more makes of computer than its rivals, it had the largest number of installations of any database system in the 1970s. In the 1980s Cincom added the MANTIS tool for building applications and SUPRA, a relational successor to TOTAL, and it stayed in private hands under its founder.`,facts:[`TOTAL was among the first database systems sold separately from computers (1968).`,`TOTAL had more installations than any other database system (1970s).`]},"company:cullinary":{intro:`Modelled on Cullinane Corporation, founded near Boston by John Cullinane, a salesman who believed companies would buy ready-made programs instead of writing everything themselves. It began by selling and supporting software written by others, such as utilities for IBM mainframes.`,how:`Cullinane licensed packaged software to large mainframe users and earned yearly maintenance fees, building one of the first sales forces devoted only to software. It looked for promising programs, bought the rights and improved them.`,history:`John Cullinane founded the company in 1968. In 1973 it bought the rights to IDMS, a database written at the tyre maker Goodrich that followed the CODASYL network standard, and IDMS became its main product. By the early 1980s IDMS was one of the most widely used databases on IBM mainframes. It renamed itself Cullinet in 1983 and added relational features as IBM's DB2 database gained ground. Computer Associates bought Cullinet in 1989.`,facts:[`IDMS followed the CODASYL committee's database standard (1971).`,`Computer Associates bought Cullinet (1989).`]},"company:softwurst":{intro:`Modelled on Software AG, founded in Darmstadt, West Germany, by Peter Schnell and five colleagues from a consultancy called AIV, the Institute for Applied Information Processing. It was one of the first software firms in Europe and set out to sell a fast database for mainframes.`,how:`Software AG licensed its ADABAS database to companies and governments running IBM mainframes, charging for licences and support. ADABAS packed records tightly, cutting the space they took by about a third, and kept indexes of every value so that searches ran quickly.`,history:`Schnell and his partners founded Software AG in 1969. It released ADABAS in 1971. Its programming language Natural, launched in 1979, let developers write database applications quickly. In 1992 Schnell set up the Software AG Foundation, a charity that held a large stake in the company. It floated in Frankfurt in 1999. The investment firm Silver Lake took it over in 2023, and in 2024 it sold its StreamSets and webMethods units to IBM.`,facts:[`ADABAS compressed records by about 30% (1971).`,`IBM paid about €2.1 billion for StreamSets and webMethods (2024).`]},"company:compacquire":{intro:`Modelled on Computer Associates, founded on Long Island, New York, by Charles Wang and Russell Artzt to sell CA-SORT, a sorting program for IBM mainframes licensed from a Swiss company. Wang, born in Shanghai, was a hard-driving salesman who believed software firms should grow by buying others.`,how:`CA sold utilities and management tools for IBM mainframes and earned steady maintenance fees on them. Wang's plan was to buy rival products along with their customers, cut costs and keep collecting the fees.`,history:`Wang and Artzt founded CA in 1976. It floated on the stock market in 1981. In 1987 it bought its big rival UCCEL, followed by Applied Data Research in 1988 and Cullinet in 1989. It bought Ingres in 1994 and Legent in 1995. In 2004 it admitted booking revenue too early, and in 2006 its former chief executive Sanjay Kumar was sentenced to 12 years in prison for fraud. Broadcom bought it for $18.9 billion in 2018.`,facts:[`CA's yearly revenue passed $1 billion (1989).`,`Broadcom paid $18.9 billion for CA (2018).`]},"company:recess":{intro:`Modelled on Digital Research, founded in Pacific Grove, California, by Gary Kildall, a computer science teacher at the Naval Postgraduate School, and his wife Dorothy McEwen, at first as Intergalactic Digital Research. Kildall had written CP/M, an operating system that let programs run on many different microcomputers built around Intel's 8080 chip.`,how:`CP/M kept the code that depended on the hardware in one small part, the BIOS, which each computer maker rewrote, so the rest of the system and all its programs worked unchanged on any machine. Digital Research licensed it to computer makers for a fee per copy.`,history:`Kildall wrote CP/M in 1974, and he and McEwen founded the company in 1976. By 1981 CP/M ran on most business microcomputers, from Z80 machines to the portable Osborne 1. In 1980 IBM approached it for an operating system for its coming PC, but no deal was struck, and IBM licensed Microsoft's DOS instead. Its GEM graphical desktop of 1985 ran on the Atari ST, and its DR-DOS of 1988 competed with MS-DOS. Novell bought Digital Research in 1991.`,facts:[`CP/M-86 for the IBM PC cost $240, six times the price of PC DOS (1982).`,`Novell paid about $80 million for Digital Research (1991).`]},"company:santacruz":{intro:`Modelled on the Santa Cruz Operation, a family business founded in Santa Cruz, California, by Larry Michels and his son Doug as a consultancy for the Unix operating system. It set out to bring Unix, the multi-user system from Bell Labs, to small business computers.`,how:`SCO began by adapting Unix to customers' machines, and its plan was to sell Unix for small, cheap computers through resellers, who bundled it with their own programs for accounting, stock control and shop tills. One computer could then serve several terminals at once.`,history:`Larry and Doug Michels founded SCO in 1979. In 1983 it released its version of Microsoft's Xenix for the IBM PC. SCO Unix followed in 1989, and SCO was the leading seller of Unix for Intel PCs in the 1990s. In 2001 it sold its Unix business to the Linux company Caldera, which renamed itself the SCO Group in 2002. In March 2003 the SCO Group sued IBM for $1 billion, claiming Linux contained stolen Unix code. In 2007 a court ruled that Novell still owned the Unix copyrights, and the SCO Group went bankrupt that year.`,facts:[`Microsoft had licensed Unix from AT&T to create Xenix (1980).`,`The SCO Group's first claim against IBM was for $1 billion (2003).`]},"company:boreland":{intro:`Modelled on Borland International, founded in Scotts Valley, California, by the French mathematician and saxophonist Philippe Kahn with three Danish partners. It sold software for personal computers by mail order at low prices, starting with a programming tool that was fast, small and cheap.`,how:`Borland sold its programs directly through magazine adverts for far less than rivals charged, betting that low prices would bring huge volume. Its products were built for speed, compiling and running programs in seconds on a modest PC.`,history:`Kahn founded Borland in 1983, and that November it launched Turbo Pascal, written by the Danish programmer Anders Hejlsberg, for $49.95. Its Sidekick desk utility of 1984 was another hit, and in 1987 it launched the Quattro spreadsheet. In 1991 it bought Ashton-Tate, the maker of dBase. Delphi, a visual tool for building Windows programs, arrived in 1995, but Hejlsberg left for Microsoft in 1996. Borland renamed itself Inprise in 1998 and went back to its old name in 2001. It sold its developer tools in 2008 and was bought by Micro Focus in 2009.`,facts:[`Turbo Pascal cost $49.95 when rival compilers cost hundreds of dollars (1983).`,`Borland paid about $440 million in shares for Ashton-Tate (1991).`]},"company:novelty":{intro:`Modelled on Novell, which began in Provo, Utah, as Novell Data Systems, a small maker of computer systems for businesses. It built multi-user machines and terminals and sold them through dealers, in a market crowded with bigger and better-funded rivals.`,how:`Novell Data Systems sold its computers and software to small businesses through dealers and made most of its money on the hardware. It was short of money and needed a better product to survive, and its strength lay in the dealers who installed and serviced systems for local firms.`,history:`Novell Data Systems was founded in Provo in 1979. The investor Ray Noorda took charge in 1983, the year it launched NetWare, which turned a PC into a file server sharing disks and printers across an office network. By 1990 Novell held more than 70% of the market for network operating systems. It bought Digital Research in 1991, the rights to Unix in 1993 and WordPerfect in 1994, but lost ground to Microsoft's Windows NT. It bought the German Linux firm SUSE in 2003. Attachmate bought Novell in 2011.`,facts:[`Novell paid about $855 million for WordPerfect (1994).`,`Attachmate paid about $2.2 billion for Novell (2011).`]},"company:banana":{intro:`Modelled on Banyan Systems, founded in Westborough, Massachusetts, by David Mahoney and other networking engineers. It set out to link the PCs and minicomputers of large organisations into one network that spanned many buildings and cities.`,how:`Banyan's network software ran on a Unix server and let PCs share files, printers and email across many sites. Its directory gave every user, printer and file server one name across the whole network, so people could find resources anywhere in a big organisation.`,history:`Banyan was founded in 1983. It launched VINES, short for Virtual Integrated Network Service, in 1984. In the 1980s VINES was popular with large organisations spread over many sites, including the US armed forces, because its StreetTalk directory made huge networks manageable. Novell's NetWare 4 of 1993 added a directory of its own, and Microsoft's Windows NT eroded Banyan's lead in the mid-1990s. It renamed itself ePresence in 1999 and turned to internet services.`,facts:[`VINES was built on AT&T's Unix System V (1984).`,`StreetTalk gave everything a three-part name: item, group and organisation (1984).`]},"company:egress":{intro:`Modelled on Relational Technology Inc, founded in Berkeley, California, by the computer scientists Michael Stonebraker, Larry Rowe and Eugene Wong to sell Ingres, the relational database they had built at the University of California. Ingres was one of the first systems to turn Edgar Codd's idea of storing data in simple tables into working software.`,how:`Relational Technology sold licences for Ingres on minicomputers such as DEC's VAX and on Unix workstations, competing with Oracle. Ingres used its own query language, QUEL, which many experts thought cleaner than IBM's SQL, and came with tools for building forms and reports.`,history:`The Ingres research project began at Berkeley in 1973, and its leaders founded Relational Technology in 1980. Ingres people helped start Sybase in 1984, and in 1986 Stonebraker began Postgres, a successor research project. In the 1980s it lost ground to Oracle, whose SQL language became the standard, and Ingres added SQL too. The company renamed itself Ingres Corporation in 1989 and was bought by the ASK Group in 1990. ASK passed it to Computer Associates in 1994. Stonebraker won the Turing Award, computing's highest prize, in 2014.`,facts:[`Computer Associates released Ingres as open source (2004).`,`Stonebraker won the Turing Award (2014).`]},"company:infomess":{intro:`Modelled on Informix, founded in Menlo Park, California, as Relational Database Systems by Roger Sippl, with Laura King, to sell database software for computers running the Unix operating system. Its databases were small enough to run on the new microcomputers and minicomputers.`,how:`Informix licensed its database and tools to companies and to makers of Unix computers, who bundled them with their machines. It aimed at smaller systems than Oracle or IBM, selling through resellers who built business applications on top.`,history:`Sippl founded the company in 1980. It renamed itself Informix in 1986, the year it floated. In 1995 it bought Illustra, Michael Stonebraker's company, for its object-relational technology, and in the mid-1990s it was the second-largest database maker after Oracle. In 1997 it admitted inflating its revenue and restated its accounts. IBM bought its database business for $1 billion in 2001, and the rest of the company was renamed Ascential Software. Its former chief executive Phil White was sentenced to prison in 2004 for his part in the fraud.`,facts:[`Informix paid about $400 million in shares for Illustra (1995).`,`IBM paid $1 billion for Informix's database business (2001).`]},"company:ashtonlate":{intro:`Modelled on Ashton-Tate, founded in Torrance, California, by George Tate and Hal Lashlee to sell a database program written by Wayne Ratliff, a contractor at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory. There was no partner called Ashton: the founders added the name because it sounded better.`,how:`Ashton-Tate sold its dBase software for personal computers through dealers, with bold, witty adverts, at prices of hundreds of dollars. dBase let business users keep records and write small programs in its own language, and thousands of developers made a living selling dBase applications.`,history:`Tate and Lashlee began selling Ratliff's program, Vulcan, in 1980. In 1981 they relaunched it as dBase II; there was never a dBase I. Its dBase III of 1984 made Ashton-Tate one of the three biggest PC software firms, but George Tate died of a heart attack that year, aged 40. Its dBase IV, released in 1988, was late and full of bugs, and customers moved to cheaper clones such as FoxBase and Clipper. In 1990 a judge threw out its copyright case against Fox Software, and in 1991 Borland bought Ashton-Tate.`,facts:[`dBase II sold for $695 (1981).`,`Borland paid about $440 million in shares for Ashton-Tate (1991).`]},"company:teradada":{intro:`Modelled on Teradata, founded in Brentwood, near Los Angeles, by a group including Jack Shemer and Phil Neches, who had studied at Caltech and worked on research for Citibank. It set out to build a database computer that split huge tables across many cheap processors working in parallel, and its name meant a trillion bytes, a size then thought fantastic.`,how:`Teradata sold complete machines, hardware and software together, to banks, retailers and airlines that needed to search enormous numbers of records. Because each processor handled its own slice of the data, adding processors made the system both bigger and faster.`,history:`Teradata was founded in 1979. In 1984 it shipped the DBC/1012, a parallel database machine whose early customers included Wells Fargo bank. AT&T bought the computer maker NCR in 1991, and NCR took over Teradata that year. In 1992 Teradata built a system for the retailer Walmart holding more than a terabyte of sales data from every store, one of the first data warehouses of that size. Teradata was spun off from NCR as an independent company in October 2007.`,facts:[`Walmart's Teradata system passed 1 terabyte (1992).`,`AT&T paid about $7.4 billion for NCR (1991).`]},"company:sighbase":{intro:`Modelled on Sybase, founded in Berkeley, California, by Mark Hoffman and Bob Epstein, an engineer from the Ingres project, to build a relational database designed for networks. Its server would run on one machine and answer requests from many desktop computers, a style known as client-server.`,how:`Sybase sold its SQL Server database for minicomputers and Unix machines. It kept programs called stored procedures inside the database and ran triggers automatically when data changed, which made it fast for busy systems such as share trading, and it licensed versions to other companies to reach more customers.`,history:`Hoffman and Epstein founded Sybase in 1984. It released SQL Server in 1987. In 1988 it agreed with Microsoft and Ashton-Tate to bring SQL Server to IBM's OS/2, and Microsoft began selling its own version in 1989. Wall Street banks adopted Sybase for trading systems in the early 1990s, but in 1994 Microsoft and Sybase ended their deal and Microsoft's version went its own way. Sybase bought the tool maker Powersoft in 1995. SAP bought Sybase for $5.8 billion in 2010.`,facts:[`Sybase paid about $940 million for Powersoft (1995).`,`SAP paid $5.8 billion for Sybase (2010).`]},"company:irrational":{intro:`Modelled on Rational Software, founded as Rational Machines by Paul Levy and Mike Devlin, US Air Force Academy graduates, to build tools for programming in Ada, the new language the US defence department required for its software. Its aim was to make huge software projects more manageable and less likely to fail.`,how:`Rational sold development environments, at first a special computer for writing Ada programs, to defence contractors and others building very large systems. It argued that software should be designed with models and built iteratively, in small tested steps, instead of in one long plan.`,history:`Levy and Devlin founded Rational Machines in 1981. In 1985 it launched the R1000, a workstation designed for Ada development. As Rational Software it merged with Verdix in 1994, and that year James Rumbaugh joined its chief scientist Grady Booch, followed in 1995 by Ivar Jacobson. The three amigos, as they were called, combined their design notations into the Unified Modeling Language, UML, which became an industry standard in 1997. It sold the Rose modelling tool, the ClearCase version-control system and the Rational Unified Process, and IBM bought it for $2.1 billion in 2003.`,facts:[`UML was adopted as a standard by the Object Management Group (1997).`,`IBM paid $2.1 billion for Rational (2003).`]},"company:microhocus":{intro:`Modelled on Micro Focus, founded in Newbury, England, by Brian Reynolds and Paul O'Grady to bring COBOL, the business programming language of mainframes, to small computers. Its first product was a COBOL compiler that ran on microcomputers.`,how:`Micro Focus sold compilers and tools that let programmers write and test COBOL on cheap desktop machines before running it on mainframes, or move old mainframe programs onto smaller computers. Its customers were banks, insurers and governments with huge collections of COBOL code.`,history:`Reynolds and O'Grady founded Micro Focus in 1976. It floated in London in 1981. In the 1980s its tools let banks and insurers develop COBOL programs on PCs and move them between mainframes and smaller computers. In 2014 it bought Attachmate, which owned Novell and SUSE. In 2017 it took over Hewlett Packard Enterprise's software business, by far its biggest deal. The Canadian firm OpenText bought Micro Focus in January 2023.`,facts:[`The HPE software deal was worth about $8.8 billion (2017).`,`OpenText paid about £5 billion for Micro Focus (2023).`]},"company:whatsnext":{intro:`Modelled on NeXT, the computer company Steve Jobs founded after being forced out of Apple, taking five Apple staff with him. It set out to build a powerful workstation for universities and students, with the best software tools and design that money could buy.`,how:`NeXT designed its computers and its own operating system, NeXTSTEP, built on Unix with ready-made software parts written in the Objective-C language, so programmers could build applications far faster. Jobs insisted on perfection, from the black magnesium case to a highly automated factory.`,history:`Jobs founded NeXT in September 1985, and Apple sued him for taking its staff. The Texan billionaire Ross Perot invested $20 million in 1987, and the NeXT Computer, a black magnesium cube, launched in October 1988 at $6,500. At CERN in 1990 Tim Berners-Lee wrote the first web browser and server on a NeXT. NeXT stopped making hardware in 1993 to concentrate on its software. Apple bought NeXT for $429 million in 1997, bringing Jobs back. Mac OS X, built on NeXTSTEP, was released in 2001.`,facts:[`Jobs paid the designer Paul Rand $100,000 for NeXT's logo (1986).`,`Ross Perot's $20 million bought about 16% of NeXT (1987).`,`id Software developed the game Doom on NeXT machines (1993).`,`NeXT sold only about 50,000 computers in all (1988 to 1993).`]},"company:pksqueeze":{intro:`Modelled on PKWARE, the company Phil Katz ran from his home near Milwaukee, Wisconsin, to sell PKARC, his faster program for squeezing files into compressed archives. Katz wrote tight assembly language, and his program ran much faster than the popular ARC program it imitated.`,how:`PKWARE sold its programs as shareware: anyone could copy and try them, and users and businesses were asked to pay a registration fee if they kept using them. Smaller files were cheaper and quicker to send over slow modems and bulletin boards, and easier to fit on floppy disks.`,history:`Katz released PKARC in 1986, a faster rival to the ARC program. In 1988 System Enhancement Associates, the makers of ARC, sued him for copying their program; he settled and agreed to stop selling PKARC. In 1989 he released PKZIP, with a new archive format, ZIP, whose description he published so that anyone could use it. ZIP soon replaced ARC on bulletin boards and was the standard way to compress files on PCs in the 1990s. Katz died in April 2000, aged 37, and PKWARE carried on in Milwaukee as a data security company.`,facts:[`Katz published the ZIP format in a text file called APPNOTE.TXT (1989).`,`PKZIP 2.0 brought in Deflate, the compression method at the heart of ZIP files (1993).`]},"company:sysenforce":{intro:`Modelled on System Enhancement Associates, a small software firm in Wayne, New Jersey, run by the programmer Thom Henderson. It wrote utility programs for personal computers and for the dial-up bulletin board systems where hobbyists swapped files and messages.`,how:`SEA wrote utility programs and sold them to businesses and computer users, often by mail and through user groups. Like many small PC software firms, it let people try its programs for free and asked them to pay if they found them useful.`,history:`System Enhancement Associates was founded in 1980. In 1985 it released ARC, a program that bundled many files into one compressed archive, which became the standard format on bulletin boards. It also wrote SEAdog, mail software for the FidoNet network of bulletin boards, in the 1980s. In 1988 it sued Phil Katz of PKWARE for copying ARC in his faster PKARC, and Katz settled. Many bulletin board users saw the case as bullying, and when Katz released PKZIP in 1989 they switched to it, leaving ARC behind.`,facts:[`ARC came out a year before Katz's faster PKARC (1985).`,`PKZIP, which replaced ARC on bulletin boards, appeared (1989).`]},"company:winzipper":{intro:`Modelled on Nico Mak Computing, the small Connecticut company founded by the programmer Nico Mak to sell WinZip, a Windows program that put a friendly point-and-click face on PKZIP's file compression. Instead of typing commands, users could open a ZIP archive and drag files in and out.`,how:`WinZip was shareware: people could use it free for a trial period, after which a reminder screen asked them to buy a licence, which many never did. Businesses and computer makers paid for site licences and bundled copies.`,history:`Mak released WinZip in April 1991, and it spread through bulletin boards, magazine cover discs and the early web, becoming one of the most downloaded programs of the 1990s. Microsoft built basic ZIP support into Windows in 2000 and 2001 as compressed folders, removing much of the need for it. The company, renamed WinZip Computing, was bought by the Canadian software firm Corel in 2006.`,facts:[`WinZip's free trial lasted 21 days (1990s).`,`WinZip 1.0 appeared in April 1991.`]},"company:stash":{intro:`Modelled on Stac Electronics, founded near San Diego by engineers from Caltech to design chips, with a focus on data compression: squeezing information so it takes less space and travels faster. Its methods found repeated patterns in data and replaced them with short references.`,how:`Stac designed compression chips and licensed its LZS compression method to makers of networking equipment and tape drives, earning royalties. Its patents covered fast ways of finding repeated patterns in a stream of data.`,history:`Stac was founded in 1983. In 1990 it launched Stacker, a program that compressed a PC's whole hard disk so that it seemed to hold about twice as much. In 1993 Microsoft added its own DoubleSpace compression to MS-DOS 6, and Stac sued for patent infringement. In February 1994 a jury awarded Stac $120 million, though Microsoft won a smaller counterclaim over trade secrets. The two settled in June 1994, with Microsoft paying royalties and buying a stake in Stac, and that year MS-DOS 6.22 replaced DoubleSpace with DriveSpace.`,facts:[`Stacker roughly doubled a hard disk's apparent capacity (1990).`,`The jury awarded Stac $120 million (1994).`]},"company:unisues":{intro:`Modelled on Unisys, formed when the computer maker Burroughs bought its rival Sperry, the owner of UNIVAC, in the largest merger the computer industry had yet seen. Its name, chosen from staff suggestions, was short for united information systems, and it set out to challenge IBM in mainframes.`,how:`Unisys sold mainframes and services to banks, airlines and governments, supporting two incompatible families of machines inherited from Burroughs and Sperry. It also owned a large stock of patents from its parents, which it licensed for fees.`,history:`Burroughs, led by Michael Blumenthal, bought Sperry for about $4.8 billion in 1986, forming Unisys. Heavy losses followed as customers moved to smaller computers, and it cut tens of thousands of jobs in the early 1990s. It owned the patent on LZW compression, invented by Sperry's Terry Welch in 1984, which the popular GIF picture format used, and in December 1994 it announced fees for software that made GIF files. The backlash led programmers to create the free PNG format in 1995. The US LZW patent expired in June 2003, and Unisys turned mainly to IT services.`,facts:[`Burroughs paid about $4.8 billion for Sperry (1986).`,`The LZW patent ran out in the United States (2003).`]},"company:frownhofer":{intro:`Modelled on the Fraunhofer Society, founded in Munich to rebuild West Germany's applied research after the war and named after Joseph von Fraunhofer, a pioneering optician and physicist. Its institutes do research for companies and governments, turning scientific discoveries into products.`,how:`Fraunhofer institutes earn most of their money from research contracts with industry and public projects, on top of a base of government funding. They patent their inventions and license them to companies, using the royalties to pay for more research.`,history:`The Fraunhofer Society was founded in Munich on 26 March 1949. Its Institute for Integrated Circuits, Fraunhofer IIS, was set up in Erlangen in 1985, and there Karlheinz Brandenburg and colleagues developed audio coding that throws away sounds the ear cannot hear. Their method became MPEG-1 Audio Layer III, approved as a standard in 1992. In July 1995 Fraunhofer chose the file extension .mp3 for it. With Thomson it licensed MP3 to the makers of players and software, and in 1997 it helped create AAC, a more efficient successor. Its MP3 licensing ended in April 2017 when the last patents ran out.`,facts:[`The .mp3 name was chosen by Fraunhofer staff on 14 July 1995.`,`Fraunhofer had about 32,000 staff in 76 institutes (2024).`]},"company:mpeglaw":{intro:`Modelled on MPEG LA, founded in Denver, Colorado, by the lawyer Baryn Futa to run patent pools: single licences covering the many patents that a video standard depends on. Instead of negotiating with dozens of patent owners, a maker of DVD players or TV set-top boxes could pay one fee to one company.`,how:`MPEG LA gathered the patents essential to a standard from many owners, such as electronics makers and universities, offered one licence at published rates and shared the royalties among the owners according to how many patents each held. It kept a share for running the pool.`,history:`MPEG LA was founded in 1996. In 1997 it launched its first pool, for the MPEG-2 video used by DVDs and digital television. In 2004 it began licensing H.264, also called AVC, which spread to Blu-ray discs and online video. In 2010 it promised that free internet video in H.264 would never be charged royalties, as Google pushed its royalty-free WebM rival. It started a pool for HEVC, or H.265, in 2013. In 2023 it merged with Via Licensing to form Via LA.`,facts:[`Its first MPEG-2 pool brought together nine patent owners, including Columbia University, Philips and Sony (1997).`,`The last US patents in its MPEG-2 pool expired (2018).`]},"company:dolbee":{intro:`Modelled on Dolby Laboratories, founded in London by Ray Dolby, an American engineer who had helped build the first video tape recorders at Ampex and then studied physics at Cambridge. Its first product was a noise-reduction system that removed the hiss from tape recordings without dulling the music.`,how:`Dolby's system boosted quiet sounds before recording and lowered them again on playback, pushing tape hiss down with them. The company sold equipment to recording studios and licensed its technology to makers of tape decks, cinema equipment and players, earning a small royalty on each one.`,history:`Ray Dolby founded the company in London in 1965. In 1966 Decca Records bought its first Dolby A noise-reduction units for its studios, and Dolby B of 1968 brought the system to cassette tapes. It moved to San Francisco in 1976, and Dolby Stereo sound helped make Star Wars a sensation in cinemas in 1977. Dolby Digital, also called AC-3, brought digital surround sound to films with Batman Returns in 1992. It floated on the stock market in 2005. In 2012 it introduced Dolby Atmos, which places sounds anywhere in a room, including overhead.`,facts:[`Dolby Digital carried 5.1 channels: five full speakers and a subwoofer (1992).`,`Dolby's revenue was about $1.3 billion (2024).`]},"company:ontoo":{intro:`Modelled on On2 Technologies, which began in New York as The Duck Corporation, founded by Dan Miller, Victor Yurkovsky and Stan Marder to make video compression software. It set out to squeeze video so that it could play smoothly from CD-ROM on ordinary computers and games consoles.`,how:`Duck licensed its video codecs, programs that compress and decompress video, to game developers and software firms, charging a fee for each product. Its methods aimed for good picture quality on slow processors.`,history:`The Duck Corporation was founded in 1992. Its TrueMotion S codec of 1995 played the video scenes in many CD-ROM and Sega Saturn games. Renamed On2, it released its VP3 codec as open source in 2001, and the Xiph.Org Foundation turned it into the free Theora format. Its VP6 was chosen for web video by Macromedia's Flash Player in 2005. Its VP8 followed in 2008. Google bought On2 in 2010 and released VP8 free that May as part of its WebM format.`,facts:[`Macromedia's Flash 8 player used its VP6 codec for web video (2005).`,`Google paid about $124.6 million in shares for On2 (2010).`]},"company:mysequel":{intro:`Modelled on MySQL AB, the Swedish company founded by Michael Widenius, David Axmark and Allan Larsson around MySQL, a fast, simple relational database that grew out of tools Widenius had written for a Swedish data firm. Widenius, known as Monty, named it after his daughter My.`,how:`MySQL AB let most people use its database free and charged companies that built it into their own products, as well as selling support and training. MySQL was quick and easy to set up, which made it popular for websites.`,history:`Widenius, Axmark and Larsson founded the company in 1995, and the first version of MySQL appeared on 23 May 1995. It moved MySQL to the GPL open-source licence in 2000. With Linux, the Apache web server and the PHP language it formed the LAMP stack that ran a huge share of websites in the 2000s, including early Facebook and Wikipedia. Sun Microsystems bought MySQL AB for $1 billion in 2008. Widenius had already forked the code as MariaDB in 2009, named after his younger daughter, by the time Oracle took over Sun and MySQL in 2010.`,facts:[`The first version of MySQL appeared on 23 May 1995.`,`Sun paid about $1 billion for MySQL AB (2008).`]},"company:sourceforgery":{intro:`Modelled on SourceForge, the free website launched by the Linux company VA Linux Systems where open-source programmers could store their code, track bugs, host downloads and work together. Any project could sign up for free, and the site earned its keep from advertising.`,how:`SourceForge gave each project a version-control repository, mailing lists, bug trackers and download mirrors around the world. Hosting thousands of projects in one place drew huge numbers of visitors, and the site made money from adverts and from promoting its owner's Linux servers.`,history:`VA Linux launched SourceForge in November 1999, a month before its own share sale broke records with a 698% first-day rise. The company renamed itself VA Software in 2001 as it gave up making computers. By 2009 SourceForge hosted hundreds of thousands of projects, and its owner had renamed itself Geeknet. GitHub arrived in 2008, and developers moved to it during the 2010s. In 2015 SourceForge was caught bundling adware with downloads of popular projects such as GIMP, and new owners, BizX, bought it in 2016 and ended the practice.`,facts:[`VA Linux's shares rose 698% on their first day of trading (1999).`,`GitHub, the rival that overtook it, opened (2008).`]},"company:jetlag":{intro:`Modelled on JetBrains, founded in Prague by three Russian programmers, Sergey Dmitriev, Valentin Kipyatkov and Eugene Belyaev, to make tools that help programmers work faster. Its first products helped developers restructure Java code safely, a task called refactoring.`,how:`JetBrains sells integrated development environments, programs in which developers write, test and debug code, that understand the code deeply enough to suggest fixes and rename things everywhere at once. It sells subscriptions and licences directly online and grew without outside investors.`,history:`JetBrains was founded in 2000 as IntelliJ. In January 2001 it released IntelliJ IDEA, a Java editor that understood code as it was typed. It added ReSharper for Microsoft's C# language in 2004 and went on to make editors for many other languages. In 2011 it announced Kotlin, its own programming language. In 2017 Google made Kotlin an official language for Android apps, whose Android Studio was itself built on IntelliJ. After Russia invaded Ukraine in 2022, JetBrains closed its offices in Russia, where many of its developers had worked.`,facts:[`Kotlin 1.0 was released (2016).`,`Google named Kotlin its preferred language for Android apps (2019).`]},"company:atlassign":{intro:`Modelled on Atlassian, founded in Sydney by two University of New South Wales graduates, Mike Cannon-Brookes and Scott Farquhar, who funded it with $10,000 of credit-card debt. They named it after Atlas, the giant of Greek myth who held up the sky, and sold software for teams of programmers.`,how:`Atlassian sold its tools online at low published prices, with free trials, instead of through a sales force, so customers could buy without talking to anyone. Its first product, Jira, tracked bugs and tasks for software teams.`,history:`Cannon-Brookes and Farquhar founded Atlassian in 2002 and released Jira that year, naming it after Gojira, the Japanese name for Godzilla, as a dig at the rival Bugzilla. The Confluence wiki, which let teams write and share documents, followed in 2004. It bought Bitbucket, a code-hosting service, in 2010. It floated on the Nasdaq exchange in December 2015. It bought the task board Trello for $425 million in 2017. In 2023 it bought the video tool Loom for about $975 million.`,facts:[`Its first share sale valued it at about $4.4 billion (2015).`,`Atlassian paid $425 million for Trello (2017).`]},"company:zensource":{intro:`Modelled on XenSource, the company founded in Cambridge, England, by Ian Pratt and colleagues from the University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory to sell support for Xen, the free hypervisor they had built there. A hypervisor runs several virtual machines on one physical server.`,how:`Xen used paravirtualisation: guest operating systems such as Linux were slightly modified to cooperate with the hypervisor, which let them run almost as fast as on bare hardware. XenSource gave Xen away as open source and sold management tools and support to businesses.`,history:`The Cambridge team first released Xen in 2003, and XenSource was founded in 2004 with offices in Cambridge and Palo Alto. Its XenEnterprise product of 2006 competed with VMware, and that year Amazon built its new EC2 cloud service on Xen. Citrix Systems bought XenSource for about $500 million in October 2007. In 2013 the Xen Project became part of the Linux Foundation.`,facts:[`Citrix paid about $500 million for XenSource (2007).`,`Amazon's EC2 ran on Xen from its launch (2006).`]},"company:mangodb":{intro:`Modelled on MongoDB Inc, which began in New York as 10gen, founded by Dwight Merriman, Eliot Horowitz and Kevin Ryan, who had built the online advertising firm DoubleClick. They first planned a cloud platform for building apps and wrote their own database for it.`,how:`MongoDB stores data as flexible documents, much like the JSON text that web programs pass around, instead of rows in fixed tables, so developers can change the shape of their data without redesigning the database. The company gave the core away as open source and sold support and enterprise features.`,history:`Merriman, Horowitz and Ryan founded 10gen in 2007. In 2009 they released its database as open source under the name MongoDB, from the word humongous. The company renamed itself MongoDB Inc in 2013. It launched Atlas, a cloud service that runs the database for customers, in 2016, and floated on the Nasdaq exchange in October 2017. In 2018 it moved to its own Server Side Public License, which stops cloud providers selling MongoDB as a service without sharing their code. Its revenue passed $2 billion in its 2025 financial year.`,facts:[`Its shares rose about 34% on their first day of trading (2017).`,`Atlas made up about 70% of its revenue (2025).`]},"company:githug":{intro:`Modelled on GitHub, the San Francisco website founded by Tom Preston-Werner, Chris Wanstrath, PJ Hyett and Scott Chacon for hosting code stored in Git, the version-control system Linus Torvalds had written for Linux. It made sharing code social: anyone could copy a project, change it and offer the changes back.`,how:`GitHub hosted public open-source projects free and charged individuals and companies for private repositories. Its pull requests let developers propose changes and discuss them line by line, and every user's profile showed their work in public.`,history:`Git was released in 2005, and GitHub opened to the public in April 2008. It grew without outside money until 2012, when Andreessen Horowitz invested $100 million. In 2018 Microsoft bought it for $7.5 billion in shares, and in 2019 it made private repositories free. In 2021 it previewed GitHub Copilot, an AI assistant built with OpenAI that suggests code as programmers type. By January 2023 it had more than 100 million developers.`,facts:[`Andreessen Horowitz's $100 million was then the firm's largest investment (2012).`,`Microsoft paid $7.5 billion in shares for GitHub (2018).`]},"company:att":{intro:`Modelled on AT&T, the American Telephone and Telegraph Company, created by the Bell Telephone Company to build the first long-distance telephone lines between cities. It grew into the head of the Bell System, which owned the local telephone companies, the Western Electric factories and the long-distance network, and was nicknamed Ma Bell.`,how:`AT&T was a regulated monopoly: it ran almost all of America's telephone service, and in return the government controlled its prices and profits. Customers rented their telephones from it, and the guaranteed income paid for its research laboratories.`,history:`AT&T was founded on 3 March 1885, became the parent of the Bell companies in 1899, opened the first transcontinental telephone line in 1915 and set up Bell Telephone Laboratories in 1925. In 1947 Bell Labs invented the transistor. In 1962 AT&T's Telstar satellite relayed the first live television across the Atlantic. In 1969 Bell Labs researchers created the Unix operating system. After a government antitrust case, AT&T agreed in 1982 to give up its local telephone companies, and the Bell System was broken up on 1 January 1984. In 2005 one of those former local companies, SBC, bought AT&T and took its name.`,facts:[`AT&T employed about 1 million people before the break-up (1983).`,`SBC paid about $16 billion for AT&T (2005).`]},"company:westeclectic":{intro:`Modelled on Western Electric, the Chicago maker of telegraph equipment that the Bell telephone company bought to be its sole manufacturer. Owned by AT&T, it made almost every telephone, switchboard, exchange and cable in the Bell System.`,how:`Western Electric built equipment to Bell's designs and sold it to the Bell telephone companies at regulated prices, so it had one huge, guaranteed customer. Its factories, such as the vast Hawthorne Works near Chicago, were among the largest in the world.`,history:`Enos Barton and Elisha Gray's electrical shop, begun in Cleveland in 1869, became the Western Electric Manufacturing Company in Chicago in 1872, and Bell bought control of it in 1881. Its Hawthorne Works gave its name to the Hawthorne studies of workers' productivity, begun there in 1924. In 1926 its sound system made Warner Brothers' first talking pictures possible, and The Jazz Singer of 1927 used its equipment. In 1951 its Allentown factory in Pennsylvania began mass-producing the transistors invented at Bell Labs. In 1956 a government consent decree forced it to license its patents to others. It became part of Lucent Technologies in 1996.`,facts:[`Its Model 500 telephone, made from 1949, sold in tens of millions.`,`Bell Labs, which it co-owned with AT&T, invented the transistor (1947).`]},"company:loosecent":{intro:`Modelled on Lucent Technologies, the equipment company AT&T spun off with most of Bell Labs and the old Western Electric factories. Based in Murray Hill, New Jersey, it made telephone exchanges, network equipment, fibre-optic gear and business phone systems for telephone companies around the world.`,how:`Lucent sold switching and transmission equipment to telephone companies and businesses, and it was free to sell to AT&T's rivals for the first time. Bell Labs gave it a huge store of patents and research, from lasers to software.`,history:`AT&T floated Lucent in April 1996, then the largest share sale in US history, and spun it off completely that September. During the dot-com boom its value peaked at about $258 billion in December 1999. When telecom spending collapsed in 2001 it lost billions of dollars and cut tens of thousands of jobs. By 2006 it had shrunk from about 150,000 staff to around 30,000. It merged with the French firm Alcatel in December 2006 to form Alcatel-Lucent. Nokia bought Alcatel-Lucent in 2016.`,facts:[`Lucent's first share sale raised about $3 billion (1996).`,`Lucent was worth about $258 billion at its peak (1999).`]},"company:babybells":{intro:`Modelled on the Baby Bells, the seven regional telephone companies created when AT&T's Bell System was broken up: Ameritech, Bell Atlantic, BellSouth, NYNEX, Pacific Telesis, Southwestern Bell and US West. Each took over the local telephone companies in one part of the United States, while AT&T kept long distance.`,how:`The Baby Bells ran local telephone lines and exchanges as regulated monopolies in their regions, earning fees from homes and businesses and from the long-distance carriers that connected to their networks. They were barred from long distance and from making equipment, but they could build mobile phone networks and publish directories.`,history:`The seven regional companies began work on 1 January 1984, each of them bigger than most American firms. The Telecommunications Act of 1996 let them enter long distance and opened their own markets to rivals. Southwestern Bell, renamed SBC, bought Pacific Telesis in 1997 and Ameritech in 1999. Bell Atlantic merged with NYNEX in 1997 and with GTE in 2000 to form Verizon, and Qwest bought US West in 2000. In 2005 SBC bought AT&T and took its name, adding BellSouth in 2006, so most of the old Bell System was back together in two companies.`,facts:[`Seven Baby Bells were created (1984); by 2006 only AT&T, Verizon and Qwest remained.`,`The Telecommunications Act let them offer long-distance calls (1996).`]},"company:verizzle":{intro:`Modelled on Verizon Communications, formed when Bell Atlantic, the regional Bell telephone company of the north-eastern United States, merged with GTE, the largest independent telephone company. Its name joined veritas, Latin for truth, and horizon, and its mobile arm, Verizon Wireless, was a joint venture with the British firm Vodafone.`,how:`Verizon ran local telephone networks in many states and a national mobile network, earning monthly fees from homes and businesses. It invested heavily in new networks, betting that customers would pay more for faster data.`,history:`Bell Atlantic and Vodafone formed Verizon Wireless in April 2000, and Bell Atlantic and GTE completed their merger that June. In 2005 Verizon launched FiOS, bringing optical fibre all the way to people's homes, and in 2006 it bought the long-distance company MCI. In December 2010 it launched America's first large-scale 4G LTE mobile network. In 2014 it paid $130 billion for Vodafone's 45% share of Verizon Wireless, one of the biggest deals ever. It bought AOL in 2015 and Yahoo's internet business in 2017. In 2021 it sold most of that media business to the investment firm Apollo.`,facts:[`Its LTE network opened in 38 cities at once (2010).`,`Verizon paid $130 billion for Vodafone's stake (2014).`]}};export{e as PEDIA_COMPANIES1};